import Foundation

enum PDLDAQuestionBank {
    static let all: [Question] = [
        Question(id: "1", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — A regional retailer has one admin who deploys everything via Change Sets with no source control, no test plan, and no documented process; deployments are frequent \"heroics\" by this single admin. The CIO wants a governance recommendation that fits the org's actual maturity, not an aspirational one. Which approach should you recommend first?", options: [("A", "Introduce Git-based source control and a basic deployment runbook, and assign a release manager, before adding CI validation or formal CAB governance."), ("B", "Stand up a full SAFe Program Increment structure with a governance board to control the admin's changes immediately."), ("C", "Migrate immediately to a package-based development model with unlocked packages to enforce modularity."), ("D", "Implement a Change Advisory Board with mandatory security and architecture review for every change, regardless of size.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: At the \"ad hoc\" ALM maturity level, the highest-leverage next step is establishing basic version control and a documented, repeatable process — this is the foundational building block that later stages (CI, CAB, packaging) depend on. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• B: SAFe PI planning assumes a program-level structure with multiple established teams; it is far too heavyweight for a single-admin ad hoc environment and will be abandoned.\n• C: Package-based development requires source control, CLI tooling, and team discipline that don't yet exist — skipping straight there ignores the maturity gap.\n• D: A formal CAB for every change is disproportionate governance overhead for a single-admin org and will simply be bypassed under delivery pressure."),
        Question(id: "2", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — A mid-sized insurer has version control and a defined branching strategy, and CI runs on every pull request, but release frequency is inconsistent and nobody tracks deployment lead time or defect escape rate. Which maturity level best characterizes this organization, and what is the appropriate next investment?", options: [("A", "This is \"ad hoc\" maturity; the next investment is introducing source control."), ("B", "This is \"defined\" maturity; the next investment is adding metrics and KPIs such as deployment frequency and lead time to move toward \"managed.\""), ("C", "This is \"optimizing\" maturity; the next investment is chaos engineering."), ("D", "This is \"repeatable\" maturity; the next investment is a documented deployment runbook.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Standardized, enforced processes (CI on every PR, defined branching) without measurement is the \"defined\" level; advancing to \"managed\" requires instrumenting the pipeline with KPIs like lead time and deployment frequency. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Ad hoc implies no consistent process at all, which contradicts the enforced CI and branching strategy already in place.\n• C: Optimizing-level practices like chaos engineering presume measured, controlled processes already exist — this org hasn't yet added metrics, so it's not there.\n• D: A documented runbook is a \"repeatable\" level action; this org has already moved past that with enforced CI and branching."),
        Question(id: "3", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — A healthcare customer operating under HIPAA wants to adopt agile delivery for faster releases but is worried about losing the audit trail their compliance team requires. As the architect, what governance recommendation best balances agile speed with regulatory requirements?", options: [("A", "Revert to waterfall exclusively, since HIPAA compliance is incompatible with agile delivery."), ("B", "Skip formal CAB review entirely and rely solely on developer self-certification of compliance for every deployment."), ("C", "Keep agile development in lower environments, but add lightweight sprint-boundary approval checkpoints and a formal CAB gate before any production deployment, with automated testing as the primary quality gate."), ("D", "Require every commit, including work-in-progress commits to feature branches, to go through full CAB approval before merging.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: This preserves auditability (who changed what, when, why) and regulatory sign-off at the point that matters — production — while letting agile iteration happen without being throttled by governance at every commit. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Waterfall is not a HIPAA requirement; many regulated organizations run agile delivery successfully with compensating governance controls.\n• B: Self-certification without any independent review removes the audit trail and accountability HIPAA-style compliance requires.\n• D: Gating every commit (not just production releases) with full CAB approval defeats the purpose of agile iteration and creates unsustainable bottleneck at the wrong level."),
        Question(id: "4", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — Three business units at a manufacturing company each independently built Salesforce customizations over several years. Technical debt is accumulating, coding standards differ wildly between teams, and duplicate custom objects have appeared. Leadership asks what structural governance mechanism would best address the root cause. What should you recommend?", options: [("A", "Hire more developers on each business unit's team to work through the technical debt backlog faster."), ("B", "Merge all three orgs into one immediately without any deduplication assessment."), ("C", "Mandate that all three teams adopt the exact same CI/CD vendor tooling within 30 days."), ("D", "Establish a Center of Excellence that owns coding standards, metadata architecture guidelines, an approved tooling list, and governance policies shared across all three teams.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [3], explanation: "Why correct: A CoE directly addresses the root cause — lack of centralized standards and shared practices across independently operating teams — without requiring an immediate, high-risk org merge. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Adding headcount without addressing standards accelerates the accumulation of inconsistent, duplicated customizations rather than resolving it.\n• B: Merging orgs without a feasibility study and deduplication plan is exactly the kind of high-risk, unplanned action a CoE is meant to prevent.\n• C: Forcing a single vendor tool in 30 days addresses tooling, not the underlying absence of shared standards, and ignores each team's context."),
        Question(id: "5", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — A program with multiple concurrent releases wants every production change to pass through a single review body regardless of size, from a one-field label update to a major integration rollout. The Release Manager complains this is creating a backlog and slowing down low-risk changes. What is the best recommendation?", options: [("A", "Remove the CAB from the process entirely and let developers deploy to production directly once tests pass."), ("B", "Introduce tiered approval gates so low-risk changes pass through automated quality gates while only high-risk or high-impact changes require full CAB review."), ("C", "Keep the single CAB body but have it meet twice as often to clear the backlog faster."), ("D", "Require the CAB to pre-approve an entire quarter's worth of changes in one sitting to save time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Risk-based tiering of approval gates matches governance overhead to actual risk, resolving the backlog for low-risk items while preserving rigorous review where it matters most. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Removing CAB entirely eliminates governance for genuinely high-risk changes, not just the low-risk ones causing the backlog.\n• C: Doubling meeting frequency treats a process design flaw as a scheduling problem and doesn't reduce the review burden on low-risk items.\n• D: Pre-approving a quarter of changes in one sitting defeats the purpose of change review, since specifics of each change aren't yet known."),
        Question(id: "6", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — Choose 2. An architect is evaluating whether a customer's current ALM approach fits a waterfall, agile, or hybrid methodology. The customer has a fixed regulatory certification deadline, stable and fully documented requirements, but multiple workstreams with varying levels of team experience. Which two factors most directly justify recommending a hybrid methodology over pure agile or pure waterfall? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "The presence of a fixed regulatory deadline with stable requirements, which favors phase-gated control typical of waterfall."), ("B", "The existence of multiple workstreams with mixed team experience levels, which benefits from agile iteration within phase boundaries."), ("C", "The customer's preference for using Jira over Azure DevOps for backlog tracking."), ("D", "The fact that the company logo uses blue as its primary brand color."), ("E", "The desire to eventually reduce the total number of Salesforce licenses purchased.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A regulatory deadline with stable, documented requirements favors waterfall-style phase gates for control and traceability, while mixed-experience workstreams benefit from agile's iterative feedback — combining both is the definition of a hybrid approach. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Tooling preference (Jira vs. Azure DevOps) is an implementation detail, not a driver of development methodology choice.\n• D: Brand color has no bearing whatsoever on ALM methodology selection.\n• E: License consolidation is a commercial/licensing consideration, unrelated to whether agile, waterfall, or hybrid delivery is appropriate."),
        Question(id: "7", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — A startup with five developers wants \"just enough\" governance to avoid chaos without slowing down its weekly release cadence. Which governance strategy best matches this profile?", options: [("A", "Full ITIL change management with a formal CMDB, risk assessments, and traceability matrices for every change."), ("B", "Lightweight governance: mandatory PR review and a basic deployment runbook, without a formal CAB."), ("C", "SAFe with Program Increments and Release Trains spanning multiple Agile Release Trains."), ("D", "No governance at all, relying entirely on individual developer judgment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Small teams with low regulatory complexity need governance proportional to their risk profile — PR review plus a basic runbook provides quality control and repeatability without the overhead of enterprise frameworks. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Full ITIL governance with CMDB and formal risk assessments is enterprise-scale overhead that a five-person team cannot sustain alongside weekly releases.\n• C: SAFe Program Increments assume multiple teams and Agile Release Trains; a five-developer startup has neither the scale nor the need for this structure.\n• D: No governance at all reintroduces the ad hoc chaos the startup explicitly wants to avoid."),
        Question(id: "8", question: "Application Lifecycle Management — After a production incident caused by an emergency change deployed without any review, leadership wants a mechanism that preserves fast turnaround for genuine emergencies while restoring accountability. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Ban all emergency changes going forward, requiring every production change to go through the standard multi-week release cycle."), ("B", "Allow any developer to self-approve emergency changes as long as they document it afterward."), ("C", "Establish a pre-authorized emergency change process with defined trigger criteria, a short list of pre-authorized approvers, and mandatory post-change retrieval and back-propagation into source control within a fixed window."), ("D", "Require the full standing CAB to convene in person for every emergency change, regardless of time of day.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: A defined emergency change process preserves speed for genuine emergencies through pre-authorized approvers while restoring accountability via mandatory documentation and back-propagation into the ALM pipeline. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Banning emergency changes entirely ignores that some production issues genuinely require faster-than-standard turnaround, and will likely be circumvented anyway.\n• B: Self-approval without independent sign-off is exactly the lack of accountability that caused the original incident.\n• D: Requiring the full CAB to convene in person for every emergency defeats the purpose of an emergency path, which needs to be fast.\n\n# Planning"),
        Question(id: "9", question: "Planning — A customer has never used version control and deploys via Change Sets, but leadership wants to jump straight to a fully automated CI/CD pipeline with unlocked packages within the next month. As the architect assessing ALM maturity, what should you recommend regarding sequencing?", options: [("A", "Proceed directly with the unlocked package and CI/CD rollout as requested, since faster tooling adoption always improves maturity."), ("B", "Recommend a phased roadmap: introduce Git source control and a sandbox hierarchy first, then branching strategy and CLI-based deployment, before layering in CI validation and package-based development."), ("C", "Skip source control but implement the CI/CD pipeline directly against the production org using Metadata API calls triggered by admin approval emails."), ("D", "Recommend the customer wait a full year before making any tooling changes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: ALM maturity improvements must be sequenced — source control and environment strategy are prerequisites for reliable CI/CD and packaging; skipping steps causes the automation to fail or be built on an unstable foundation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Jumping to CI/CD and packaging without source control and sandbox discipline in place means there is nothing reliable for the pipeline to build from.\n• C: Automating deployments straight to production without source control or testing gates removes the very safety net CI/CD is meant to add.\n• D: A full year of inaction ignores that meaningful, sequenced progress can start immediately with source control adoption."),
        Question(id: "10", question: "Planning — An architect reviews a customer's environment and finds admins routinely making config changes directly in the sandbox used for UAT, without retrieving those changes into source control, causing UAT to diverge from what's deployed to production. What is the most direct mitigation?", options: [("A", "Disable the UAT sandbox entirely until the project ends."), ("B", "Establish a 'retrieve and commit' workflow requiring any declarative change made in a sandbox to be retrieved into source control before the sandbox is refreshed or the change is considered complete."), ("C", "Increase the UAT sandbox refresh frequency to daily so drift is reset constantly."), ("D", "Require all UAT testers to memorize the differences and manually document them in a spreadsheet after each session.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The direct root cause is that declarative changes aren't being captured in source control; a retrieve-and-commit workflow closes that gap and prevents configuration drift from ever accumulating. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Disabling the sandbox halts UAT testing entirely rather than fixing the underlying process gap.\n• C: Refreshing more frequently would destroy in-progress UAT work and does not address why changes aren't being captured in source control.\n• D: Manual spreadsheet tracking doesn't fix drift — it just documents it after the fact, and is error-prone at scale."),
        Question(id: "11", question: "Planning — A customer's Full Copy staging sandbox contains unmasked production PII, and any developer with sandbox access can view real customer data including SSNs. Compliance flags this as a critical risk. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Downgrade the Full Copy sandbox to a Developer sandbox to eliminate the risk entirely."), ("B", "Apply data masking or anonymization to the sandbox before granting broad developer access, and restrict truly unmasked Full Copy access to a small group of authorized personnel only."), ("C", "Grant everyone access since the sandbox is not production and therefore not subject to compliance rules."), ("D", "Encrypt the entire sandbox database at rest, which is sufficient to satisfy data privacy requirements for developer access.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Masking sensitive fields before granting broad access directly closes the data leakage risk while preserving the realistic, production-representative data volume Full Copy sandboxes are used for. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Downgrading loses the production-scale data needed for performance/staging testing and doesn't address the actual masking requirement for any environment holding real data.\n• C: Non-production environments containing real PII are still subject to the same data privacy compliance obligations as production.\n• D: Encryption at rest protects against external theft of the database file, not against authorized developers viewing live PII on screen — it doesn't substitute for masking."),
        Question(id: "12", question: "Planning — A customer with three independent project streams shares a single integration sandbox. Each week, one team's changes overwrite another's during deployment, and root cause is unclear because there's no test isolation. What is the best mitigation strategy?", options: [("A", "Allocate dedicated developer sandboxes per team, use feature branches merged only when ready, and validate each branch in CI before merging into a scheduled integration sandbox refresh."), ("B", "Merge all three teams into a single unified backlog so there is only one stream of work."), ("C", "Have the three teams take turns deploying on alternating days of the week without any other process change."), ("D", "Disable sandbox refreshes entirely so no team's work is ever lost.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Isolating each team's development in dedicated sandboxes and validating merges via CI before they reach the shared integration environment directly eliminates the collision and attribution problems described. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• B: Merging backlogs doesn't solve the technical collision problem of shared sandbox metadata overwrites; it's an organizational, not architectural, fix.\n• C: Alternating days without isolation or validation doesn't prevent overwrites, it just changes their timing.\n• D: Disabling refreshes entirely doesn't address root-cause metadata collisions and eventually causes the sandbox to diverge badly from production."),
        Question(id: "13", question: "Planning — A UAT sandbox hasn't been refreshed in 10 months while production has had six releases in that time. Testers report tests failing due to stale configuration, and nobody can tell if failures are real defects or environment drift. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Have testers manually reconfigure the sandbox to match production every time a test fails."), ("B", "Define a sandbox refresh schedule aligned to release cadence (e.g., refresh UAT after each production release), automate post-refresh steps, and track sandbox age with alerts when it exceeds a threshold."), ("C", "Stop using UAT sandboxes altogether and test only in production going forward."), ("D", "Refresh the UAT sandbox only once a year regardless of release cadence, to keep costs down.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Aligning refresh cadence to release cycles, automating the post-refresh setup, and tracking sandbox age directly resolves the drift causing indistinguishable test failures. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Manual reconfiguration on every failure is unsustainable and doesn't establish a repeatable, scheduled process.\n• C: Testing directly in production eliminates the safety of a pre-production gate and introduces unacceptable risk to real customers and data.\n• D: An annual refresh cadence is far too infrequent relative to six production releases in ten months and guarantees continued drift."),
        Question(id: "14", question: "Planning — Choose 2. A customer engaged three vendors on a single Salesforce program without a clear release coordination model. Vendor A deployed a component that broke Vendor B's integration, and no one could determine who owned the shared release calendar or the code repository. Which two governance actions should the architect recommend? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Designate a vendor-neutral release manager (typically customer staff) who owns a shared release calendar visible to all vendors."), ("B", "Ensure the customer owns the source control repository and CI/CD pipeline, with vendor teams contributing via pull requests rather than deploying directly to production."), ("C", "Let each vendor maintain its own separate, private repository with no cross-visibility to protect vendor IP."), ("D", "Allow whichever vendor deploys first each week to have deployment priority, decided informally."), ("E", "Require all three vendors to use the exact same page layout naming convention.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A vendor-neutral release manager with a shared calendar and centralized, customer-owned source control with PR-based contribution are the structural fixes that establish clear ownership and prevent conflicting, uncoordinated deployments. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Separate private repositories with no cross-visibility is precisely what caused the conflict — it prevents any coordination between vendors.\n• D: Informal first-come deployment priority is not a governance mechanism and doesn't prevent future conflicts.\n• E: A shared naming convention for layouts is a coding standard detail, not a structural fix for multi-vendor release coordination."),
        Question(id: "15", question: "Planning — A multi-vendor Salesforce program is ending its contract with one vendor. The customer discovers that vendor-developed Apex code and metadata live only in that vendor's private Git repository, and the customer has no rights to access it going forward. What planning step would have prevented this?", options: [("A", "Requiring code reviews on every pull request during development."), ("B", "Contractually defining that the customer owns the source control repository and retains full IP rights to custom metadata and Apex code regardless of which vendor authored it."), ("C", "Requiring the vendor to use Salesforce DX project format for their codebase."), ("D", "Mandating that the vendor use scratch orgs instead of sandboxes for development.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: IP ownership and repository custody must be contractually defined upfront; owning the repository ensures the customer retains access and rights to all code regardless of vendor relationship changes. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Code review process quality has no bearing on who legally owns the resulting code or where it's stored.\n• C: Using SFDX project format is a technical convention and doesn't establish IP ownership or repository access rights.\n• D: Scratch org usage is a development environment choice unrelated to contractual IP and repository ownership."),
        Question(id: "16", question: "Planning — A customer is six weeks away from a Salesforce seasonal release upgrade to production. The architect wants to proactively identify risk areas. Which approach is most effective?", options: [("A", "Wait until the day of the production upgrade to test, since Salesforce guarantees backward compatibility."), ("B", "Analyze release notes for 'Enforce by default' and 'Critical Update' items, deploy to a preview sandbox during the release preview window, and run the full regression suite plus targeted testing on flagged features."), ("C", "Only test features the customer plans to build in the next quarter, ignoring existing functionality."), ("D", "Rely exclusively on Salesforce's own internal testing and skip customer-side regression testing altogether.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Combining release notes analysis with hands-on preview sandbox testing and full regression is the standard proactive risk-mitigation approach for seasonal releases, surfacing issues weeks before the mandatory production upgrade. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Salesforce does not guarantee zero-impact upgrades — behavior changes, enforced defaults, and API changes can break existing customizations.\n• C: Ignoring existing functionality misses the primary risk, since seasonal releases most often affect current customizations, not unbuilt future features.\n• D: Salesforce's internal testing covers platform-level quality, not customer-specific customizations, integrations, or Apex — customer-side regression testing is essential."),
        Question(id: "17", question: "Planning — A customer wants to opt out of the sandbox release preview upgrade for their UAT sandbox because they have a critical UAT cycle in progress and don't want the seasonal release changing behavior mid-cycle. What is the key planning constraint they must account for?", options: [("A", "Opt-out requests can be submitted at any time, even after the sandbox has already been upgraded."), ("B", "The opt-out window is limited and must be requested from Salesforce support before a defined cutoff date, typically a few weeks before the sandbox upgrade — missing it means the sandbox will upgrade on schedule."), ("C", "Only Full Copy sandboxes are eligible for release preview, so this sandbox would not be affected regardless."), ("D", "Opting out of preview also delays the production org's upgrade by the same amount of time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The opt-out window for sandbox release preview is time-limited and must be requested proactively through Salesforce support before the cutoff — this is the key planning constraint the customer needs to know to protect their UAT cycle. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Opt-out requests cannot be made retroactively after the sandbox has already upgraded; the window closes before the scheduled upgrade.\n• C: Developer, Developer Pro, and Partial Copy sandboxes typically receive preview; Full Copy sandboxes are the ones that typically do NOT receive the preview upgrade — the reverse of this option's claim.\n• D: Opting a sandbox out of preview has no effect on the production org's own mandatory upgrade schedule."),
        Question(id: "18", question: "Planning — Following a seasonal release preview test cycle, the architect discovers that several existing Apex unit tests now fail due to a platform behavior change flagged as a Critical Update (Release Update) with a mandatory auto-activation date six weeks away. What is the correct planning response?", options: [("A", "Ignore the failures since Critical Updates can always be deferred indefinitely with no consequence."), ("B", "Opt in to the update now in a sandbox, fix the failing Apex logic and tests proactively, and deploy the fix to production before the mandatory auto-activation date."), ("C", "Wait until the auto-activation date passes and address any resulting production incidents reactively."), ("D", "File a support case asking Salesforce to permanently exempt the org from ever activating the update.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Because auto-activation is time-bound and unavoidable, proactively fixing and deploying the corrected logic before the deadline avoids a production incident and keeps the fix within the normal ALM pipeline. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Release Updates (formerly Critical Updates) auto-activate on a fixed timeline; they cannot be deferred indefinitely.\n• C: Waiting for auto-activation to cause a production incident is the exact failure mode proactive planning is meant to avoid.\n• D: Salesforce does not offer permanent exemptions from mandatory Release Update auto-activation; deferral is time-limited, not indefinite."),
        Question(id: "19", question: "Planning — A CI pipeline deploys using a hardcoded, older Salesforce API version in its scripts. The upcoming seasonal release is expected to deprecate that API version. What risk should the architect flag to the delivery team, and what is the fix?", options: [("A", "No risk exists, since Salesforce CLI automatically upgrades API versions referenced in pipeline scripts without any changes needed."), ("B", "The pipeline deployments may begin failing once the API version is deprecated; the fix is to update the API version referenced in pipeline scripts and metadata before the release, and validate this in the preview sandbox."), ("C", "The only risk is a minor UI warning banner in Setup; no functional impact will occur."), ("D", "This risk only affects package-based development, not org-based CI pipelines using Metadata API deployments.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Deprecated API versions can cause deployment failures once fully retired, so proactively updating the API version in pipeline scripts and metadata, then validating against the preview sandbox, prevents a broken pipeline at release time. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Salesforce CLI does not automatically rewrite API version references embedded in a customer's own deployment metadata and scripts.\n• C: A deprecated API version can cause actual deployment failures, not merely a cosmetic warning.\n• D: This risk applies to any deployment mechanism referencing a specific API version, including org-based Metadata API deployments, not just packages."),
        Question(id: "20", question: "Planning — Business stakeholders are frustrated because they were surprised by UI behavior changes after a Salesforce seasonal release went to production, despite the technical team having tested it thoroughly in the preview sandbox. What planning gap does this reveal?", options: [("A", "The technical testing was insufficient and should be repeated with more test cases."), ("B", "There was no non-technical release impact communication to business stakeholders describing what would change, when, and what actions they needed to take, and no stakeholder-facing UAT cycle scheduled before the production upgrade window."), ("C", "The seasonal release should have been declined entirely, since seasonal releases are optional for customers."), ("D", "The preview sandbox window was too short to catch the change, so Salesforce should be asked to extend it indefinitely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Even thorough technical testing doesn't address stakeholder communication — a non-technical release impact summary and a pre-upgrade stakeholder UAT cycle are the specific planning steps that prevent business-side surprises. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: The scenario states technical testing was thorough; the gap is communication and stakeholder involvement, not test coverage.\n• C: Salesforce seasonal releases are mandatory platform-wide upgrades for all customers; declining them entirely is not an available option.\n• D: The preview window length isn't the root cause here — the technical team already caught the change; the failure was in cascading that information to the business."),
        Question(id: "21", question: "Planning — A company is preparing a formal, evidence-based governance framework proposal for a new enterprise-wide Salesforce program spanning five business units, each with different SLAs and compliance obligations. Leadership wants a data-driven audit trail for every change. Which framework should the architect most likely recommend as the primary foundation?", options: [("A", "A lightweight PR-review-only process with no formal change records."), ("B", "ITIL-based governance, given its strength in formal change management, incident management, and audit-trail-oriented CMDB practices suited to SLA- and compliance-driven, IT-operations-heavy environments."), ("C", "Pure Kanban with no defined release cadence or approval gates."), ("D", "A single informal Slack channel where any team member can approve any other team's change.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: ITIL's formal change management, incident management, and audit-trail-oriented practices directly match the requirement for SLA compliance and a data-driven, auditable governance foundation across multiple business units. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A PR-review-only process lacks the formal, cross-business-unit change and incident tracking the compliance requirement demands.\n• C: Pure Kanban with no approval gates provides workflow visualization but no formal audit trail or compliance-oriented change control.\n• D: An informal approval channel provides no structured audit trail and no segregation of duties required for compliance obligations.\n\n# System Design"),
        Question(id: "22", question: "System Design — A Salesforce delivery team currently tracks work in spreadsheets emailed weekly. Leadership wants better cross-team visibility into sprint progress and impediments without adding heavy process. What is the most direct benefit of introducing an agile project management tool like Jira or Azure Boards in this context?", options: [("A", "It eliminates the need for sprint planning meetings entirely."), ("B", "It automatically writes Apex unit tests based on backlog descriptions."), ("C", "It provides shared visibility into work in progress, velocity, and impediments without requiring additional status meetings, and enables backlog prioritization across teams."), ("D", "It replaces the need for a sandbox environment strategy.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Agile tools centralize backlog, sprint, and impediment visibility so stakeholders and teams get real-time status without manual spreadsheet updates or extra meetings — directly solving the stated visibility problem. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Agile tools support sprint planning; they don't eliminate the need for the planning conversation itself.\n• B: No agile tracking tool generates Apex test code from backlog text; this conflates project tracking with code generation.\n• D: Sprint tracking tools and sandbox environment strategy address entirely different concerns; one doesn't substitute for the other."),
        Question(id: "23", question: "System Design — A customer wants full traceability from a user story to the exact metadata deployed to production, including which developer wrote it, which tests passed, and who approved the deployment. Which combination of practices best achieves this?", options: [("A", "Rely on the Release Manager's memory and informal Slack threads to reconstruct history when needed."), ("B", "Link Git commits to backlog tickets using smart commit syntax, have CI post build/test results back to the ticket, and use a deployment tool that ties user stories to specific deployed components."), ("C", "Store all user stories only as PDF attachments in email, separate from any tooling."), ("D", "Require developers to manually re-type ticket numbers into a spreadsheet after each deployment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Linking commits to tickets, automating CI status back to the ticket, and using deployment tooling that maps stories to components creates an automated, auditable chain from requirement to deployed artifact. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Relying on memory and informal chat threads provides no reliable, auditable trace and fails under any real compliance scrutiny.\n• C: PDF attachments disconnected from any tooling cannot be automatically linked to commits, builds, or deployments.\n• D: Manual re-typing introduces human error and isn't a scalable or reliable traceability mechanism."),
        Question(id: "24", question: "System Design — A company recently acquired a competitor that runs its own separate Salesforce org with overlapping Account and Contact data and a different sales process. Leadership wants a recommendation on org strategy within 90 days. What should the architect propose first?", options: [("A", "Immediately merge the two orgs into one within the 90-day window regardless of data overlap."), ("B", "Conduct a formal feasibility study evaluating data overlap, process compatibility, technical complexity, and licensing before deciding between merging or having the orgs coexist."), ("C", "Permanently maintain both orgs as separate silos with no future evaluation, since merging orgs is always too risky."), ("D", "Immediately delete all data in the acquired company's org to avoid deduplication work.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Merging orgs is a significant undertaking with real risk of data loss and political friction from process standardization; a structured feasibility study is the appropriate first step before committing to either merge or coexist. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Merging within 90 days without assessing data overlap and process compatibility risks major data corruption and business disruption.\n• C: Permanently avoiding evaluation ignores that coexistence has real long-term costs (duplicate licensing, fragmented reporting) that may outweigh merge risk.\n• D: Deleting data without deduplication analysis destroys potentially critical business records and is not a legitimate strategy."),
        Question(id: "25", question: "System Design — A single Salesforce org supporting a high-volume contact center is regularly hitting synchronous SOQL query limits and CPU time limits during peak hours due to integration volume. What architectural approach should the architect evaluate first, before considering a multi-org split?", options: [("A", "Immediately split the org into two separate orgs to spread the transaction volume."), ("B", "Move high-volume, non-time-sensitive processing to asynchronous patterns (Queueable/Batch Apex, Platform Events) to flatten peak load within the existing single org before considering a more disruptive multi-org split."), ("C", "Increase the number of System Administrator licenses to handle the load."), ("D", "Disable all validation rules to reduce CPU time consumption during peak hours.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Async processing patterns directly address governor limit pressure caused by synchronous peak load without the massive organizational and technical cost of a multi-org split, which should be reserved for cases where single-org architecture genuinely cannot scale further. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Splitting into multiple orgs is a drastic, costly step that introduces significant integration and governance complexity, and should only be considered after less disruptive options are exhausted.\n• C: Adding admin licenses has no effect on governor limits or transaction volume; it addresses a staffing concern, not a technical one.\n• D: Disabling validation rules removes data integrity protections and does not meaningfully address CPU time consumption from the actual high-volume integration processing."),
        Question(id: "26", question: "System Design — Choose 2. An architect is deciding between a single-org and multi-org strategy for a company with two business units that share almost no customers, processes, or reporting needs, and one unit operates under separate regulatory data residency requirements. Which two factors most strongly justify a multi-org approach here? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Regulatory data residency requirements mandating data separation between the business units."), ("B", "Near-total lack of shared customers, processes, or reporting needs between the business units."), ("C", "Both business units use the same corporate email domain."), ("D", "Both business units want to use Lightning Experience instead of Salesforce Classic."), ("E", "Both business units have fewer than 50 total users combined.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Regulatory data residency requirements are a hard driver for data isolation, and a near-total lack of shared data or process needs removes most of the benefit single-org architecture would otherwise provide (shared reporting, unified data model) — together these justify separate orgs. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Sharing an email domain is a corporate branding detail with no bearing on data architecture decisions.\n• D: Lightning Experience is an interface choice available in either single-org or multi-org configurations; it doesn't drive the org-split decision.\n• E: A small combined user count actually argues against the added operational overhead of multi-org, since governor limits and licensing costs are unlikely to be a constraint at that scale."),
        Question(id: "27", question: "System Design — A customer with five parallel development streams (three feature teams, a hotfix team, and a training initiative) asks you to design a sandbox environment hierarchy for an upcoming major release. Which structure best supports this without excessive risk of collision or drift?", options: [("A", "A single shared Developer sandbox for all five streams to save on sandbox costs."), ("B", "Individual Developer sandboxes per feature team, a shared Integration sandbox for merged work, a Partial Copy QA sandbox, a Partial Copy UAT sandbox, a Full Copy Staging sandbox, a dedicated hotfix sandbox kept near production parity, and a separate training sandbox refreshed only from UAT-approved builds."), ("C", "One Full Copy sandbox shared by all teams including training, refreshed nightly."), ("D", "No sandboxes at all; all five streams develop directly against production behind feature flags.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This hierarchy isolates each feature team to prevent collisions, provides progressive convergence toward production-like environments for testing, keeps the hotfix path separate for emergency changes, and protects training from in-flight development churn. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A single shared Developer sandbox across five parallel streams guarantees the exact metadata collision and attribution problems dedicated sandboxes are meant to prevent.\n• C: Sharing one Full Copy sandbox across all streams including training mixes unreleased development work into the training environment and creates constant refresh conflicts (Full Copy refresh cycles are also much longer, at 29 days).\n• D: Developing directly against production abandons any pre-production testing gate and is far riskier than even a minimal sandbox strategy."),
        Question(id: "28", question: "System Design — A team currently develops entirely in a single shared Full Copy sandbox and refreshes it only every 29 days, which is causing long waits whenever a developer needs a clean environment for a new feature. What environment change would most directly relieve this bottleneck for individual feature development, without abandoning production-like testing entirely?", options: [("A", "Ask Salesforce support to reduce the Full Copy refresh interval to daily for this org."), ("B", "Introduce Developer or Developer Pro sandboxes (refreshable every day) for individual feature development, reserving the Full Copy sandbox for staging/pre-production validation only."), ("C", "Have every developer share login credentials to the same Developer sandbox to reduce the number of environments needed."), ("D", "Eliminate the Full Copy sandbox entirely and do all testing, including staging validation, in scratch orgs.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Developer/Developer Pro sandboxes refresh every day and are purpose-built for individual feature work, relieving the wait time, while preserving the Full Copy sandbox's role for production-equivalent staging validation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Salesforce does not allow arbitrary reduction of the Full Copy sandbox's fixed 29-day refresh interval.\n• C: Shared credentials on one sandbox reintroduces the same collision problem the team is trying to avoid and provides no real isolation.\n• D: Removing the Full Copy sandbox eliminates the production-equivalent data volume and configuration needed for meaningful staging and performance validation."),
        Question(id: "29", question: "System Design — A customer's QA sandbox refresh always resets custom setting values and unassigns permission sets that testers depend on, delaying every QA cycle by a day while admins manually reconfigure. How should the architect address this recurring pain point?", options: [("A", "Stop refreshing the QA sandbox altogether to avoid the reconfiguration work."), ("B", "Automate post-refresh setup steps (custom setting values, permission set assignments, test data seeding) as a scripted step that runs immediately after every scheduled refresh."), ("C", "Assign System Administrator profile to all QA testers so permission sets are no longer needed."), ("D", "Move QA testing into the Staging Full Copy sandbox instead, since it never needs to be refreshed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Automating the post-refresh configuration steps removes the recurring manual delay while preserving the benefit of a regularly refreshed, production-aligned QA environment. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Never refreshing the QA sandbox reintroduces configuration drift and stale data, trading one problem for a worse one.\n• C: Granting System Administrator to all testers bypasses the permission-based testing QA is meant to validate, undermining the purpose of the test cycle.\n• D: Full Copy sandboxes also require refreshing (every 29 days) and post-refresh reconfiguration; moving testing there doesn't eliminate the underlying automation gap."),
        Question(id: "30", question: "System Design — A team currently uses Change Sets exclusively. They now need to delete three deprecated custom fields as part of an upcoming release, and also want an audit trail of exactly what was deployed and when. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Continue using Change Sets, and manually delete the deprecated fields directly in production after the Change Set deployment completes."), ("B", "Migrate this deployment to Salesforce CLI using a package with a destructiveChanges.xml for the field deletions, tracked in source control to provide the audit trail Change Sets cannot offer."), ("C", "Use the ANT Migration Tool exclusively going forward, since it is the modern standard for all Salesforce deployments."), ("D", "Request that Salesforce support manually delete the fields in production on the team's behalf.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Change Sets cannot perform destructive changes in the same deployment and provide no deployment history; CLI deployments with a destructiveChanges.xml, tracked in source control, solve both the deletion requirement and the audit trail need. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Manually deleting fields directly in production after a Change Set deploy bypasses any tracked, auditable process and risks human error with no rollback record.\n• C: The ANT Migration Tool is a legacy tool that has been superseded by Salesforce CLI; it is not the modern recommended standard.\n• D: Salesforce support does not perform ad hoc metadata deletions on a customer's behalf as a substitute for the customer's own deployment tooling."),
        Question(id: "31", question: "System Design — Choose 2. A team is choosing a CI/CD platform for a new Salesforce program. They are already on GitHub for source control, want strong Salesforce CLI community support, and have no existing Jenkins infrastructure or dedicated DevOps engineers to maintain one. Which two factors should most strongly influence the recommendation toward GitHub Actions over Jenkins? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "GitHub Actions is native to GitHub repositories and has strong Salesforce community-maintained actions, reducing integration friction."), ("B", "The team lacks the infrastructure and dedicated staff typically needed to self-host and maintain Jenkins."), ("C", "Jenkins cannot technically run Salesforce CLI commands under any configuration."), ("D", "GitHub Actions guarantees zero cost regardless of usage volume for any team size."), ("E", "The team's preferred programming language is Apex.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Native GitHub integration with community-supported Salesforce actions minimizes setup friction, and the absence of Jenkins infrastructure or dedicated staff makes a self-hosted Jenkins deployment a poor operational fit — both point toward GitHub Actions. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Jenkins can absolutely run Salesforce CLI commands via shell steps in a Jenkinsfile; this claim is factually incorrect.\n• D: GitHub Actions has usage-based billing beyond a free tier; it is not unconditionally free at every usage volume.\n• E: Apex being the team's Salesforce language has no bearing on which CI/CD orchestration platform (which runs shell/CLI commands, not Apex itself) is appropriate."),
        Question(id: "32", question: "System Design — A customer wants to move off Change Sets but has a small admin-heavy team, a limited budget for third-party DevOps tooling, and is already using GitHub as their source of truth. Which native Salesforce release management option best fits this profile?", options: [("A", "Salesforce DevOps Center, which provides a guided GUI experience with GitHub-based source control integration, pipeline visualization, and work item tracking without third-party licensing costs."), ("B", "Copado, given its enterprise-grade governance and AI-assisted features."), ("C", "A fully custom Jenkins pipeline built from scratch by the admin team."), ("D", "Continue using Change Sets but add a shared spreadsheet to track what was deployed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: DevOps Center is Salesforce-native, integrates with GitHub (already their SCM), provides a guided GUI suited to admin-heavy teams without deep CLI/CI expertise, and has no additional per-seat third-party licensing cost. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• B: Copado is a strong enterprise platform but adds licensing cost this budget-constrained team explicitly wants to avoid.\n• C: Building a custom Jenkins pipeline from scratch requires DevOps expertise this admin-heavy team does not have, contradicting the stated constraint.\n• D: A spreadsheet doesn't address any of the core Change Set limitations (no destructive changes, no automated testing, no versioning) and isn't a genuine tooling upgrade."),
        Question(id: "33", question: "System Design — During a production deployment, an Apex class references a custom field that has not yet been deployed, causing the deployment to fail with a dependency error. The team deploys components individually rather than as a single package. What is the best architectural fix?", options: [("A", "Rename the custom field to something unrelated so the dependency error no longer appears."), ("B", "Bundle all interdependent components (the custom field and the Apex class that references it) into a single deployment package so the Metadata API can resolve dependencies together, or sequence foundational components first."), ("C", "Convert the custom field to a formula field, which does not require dependency resolution."), ("D", "Disable the Apex class temporarily and redeploy it manually after the field is confirmed to exist.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Metadata API deployments must respect component dependency order; bundling dependent components into a single deployment (or explicitly sequencing foundational components first) is the standard fix for this class of dependency failure. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Renaming the field does not address the underlying dependency ordering issue and would break other references to the original field name.\n• C: Formula fields are still subject to the same dependency resolution rules as any other custom field; converting field type does not bypass this.\n• D: Manually disabling and redeploying the Apex class separately doesn't establish a repeatable process and reintroduces the same risk on every future deployment involving dependencies."),
        Question(id: "34", question: "System Design — Choose 2. An architect is designing a CI/CD pipeline for a Salesforce program that must run Apex tests, static analysis, and metadata deployment as separate stages. Which two capabilities are essential for the pipeline to reliably gate promotion to production? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A validation-only deployment stage that runs tests against the target org without committing changes, used to confirm deployability before a real deploy."), ("B", "A static analysis stage (e.g., PMD or Salesforce Code Analyzer) that can fail the build on critical violations introduced in the change."), ("C", "A stage that automatically increases the org's API request limit for the day."), ("D", "A stage that renames all Apex classes to remove vowels for faster compilation."), ("E", "A stage that permanently disables all validation rules in the target org before every deployment.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A validation-only deploy stage confirms deployability and test pass rate without side effects, and a static analysis gate catches code-quality regressions before they reach production — both are standard, essential quality gates in a Salesforce CI/CD pipeline. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: There is no pipeline stage that increases an org's daily API limit; this is not a real capability.\n• D: Renaming classes to remove vowels is nonsensical and would break every reference to those classes; it has no relationship to compilation speed.\n• E: Permanently disabling validation rules removes real data integrity protections and is a governance anti-pattern, not a legitimate pipeline stage."),
        Question(id: "35", question: "System Design — A team's current package-based project has three unlocked packages: a Utilities package with shared Apex helper classes, a Billing package that calls those helpers, and a Reporting package that depends on Billing. During a deployment, Reporting fails to install because Billing hasn't been installed yet in the target org. What is the correct architectural fix?", options: [("A", "Merge all three packages into a single package to eliminate dependency management entirely."), ("B", "Declare the dependency relationships explicitly in sfdx-project.json (Reporting depends on Billing, Billing depends on Utilities) so the tooling installs packages in the correct order automatically."), ("C", "Rename the Reporting package so it alphabetically sorts before Billing, forcing earlier installation."), ("D", "Remove the dependency on Billing from Reporting's code without changing any functionality.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Explicitly declaring package dependencies in sfdx-project.json lets Salesforce CLI resolve and enforce the correct installation order automatically, which is exactly the mechanism designed to prevent this class of failure. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Merging all packages into one eliminates the modular, independently releasable architecture the team presumably chose unlocked packages to achieve in the first place.\n• C: Alphabetical naming has no effect on Salesforce CLI's package installation order, which is driven by declared dependencies, not naming.\n• D: Removing the dependency without changing functionality is not actually possible if Reporting's code genuinely calls Billing's components; this would break the application."),
        Question(id: "36", question: "System Design — A customer's current deployment approach uses raw Metadata API calls scripted by hand, and the developer who understands the dependency ordering is leaving the company. Leadership wants to reduce reliance on tribal knowledge for handling deployment dependencies going forward. What is the best long-term recommendation?", options: [("A", "Document the departing developer's knowledge in a single onboarding email and hope future developers read it."), ("B", "Adopt a DevOps tool (e.g., Copado, Gearset) or Salesforce DevOps Center that automatically resolves and manages component dependency order, reducing reliance on any one individual's manual knowledge."), ("C", "Ask the departing developer to stay on call indefinitely after leaving to answer dependency questions."), ("D", "Freeze all further deployments until a replacement developer with equivalent tribal knowledge is hired.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Tooling that automates dependency resolution converts tribal knowledge into a repeatable, tool-enforced process, directly reducing single-person-dependency risk going forward. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A single onboarding email is not a durable knowledge transfer mechanism and doesn't scale as the team or metadata complexity grows.\n• C: Relying on an indefinite on-call arrangement with a former employee is not a sustainable or professionally realistic risk mitigation.\n• D: Freezing all deployments indefinitely halts business value delivery entirely rather than addressing the actual knowledge-transfer gap.\n\n# Building"),
        Question(id: "37", question: "Building — A high-maturity Salesforce team commits to a single main branch multiple times daily, using feature flags to hide incomplete work, and relies on strong automated test coverage. A new architect proposes switching to Gitflow with long-lived develop, release, and hotfix branches. What should you advise?", options: [("A", "Adopt Gitflow immediately, since long-lived branches are always safer than trunk-based development regardless of team maturity."), ("B", "Keep trunk-based development, since it already fits this team's high test automation maturity and frequent integration cadence; switching to Gitflow would introduce longer-lived branches and higher merge conflict risk without a corresponding benefit."), ("C", "Adopt Gitflow only for hotfixes while keeping trunk-based development for everything else, without any team discussion."), ("D", "Abandon branching entirely and have all developers commit directly to production metadata.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Trunk-based development is well-suited to teams with strong test automation and frequent integration; introducing Gitflow's long-lived branches would raise merge conflict risk in large metadata files without addressing any actual problem this team has. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Gitflow's long-lived branches are not universally safer — they increase merge conflict risk in Salesforce's large XML metadata files, which is a real cost for high-maturity teams.\n• C: Mixing branching models ad hoc without evaluating trade-offs or aligning the team creates confusion rather than addressing an actual limitation of trunk-based development.\n• D: Eliminating branching entirely removes any isolation for in-progress work and is not a serious source control strategy."),
        Question(id: "38", question: "Building — A critical production bug is found while a large feature branch is mid-development and not yet ready to release. The team needs to ship a hotfix without releasing the incomplete feature. What is the correct branching approach?", options: [("A", "Merge the entire feature branch into production early just to access the hotfix code needed."), ("B", "Create the hotfix branch from the production release tag (not from the in-progress feature or develop branch), fix and test it there, deploy it to production, then merge the hotfix back into develop and all active feature branches to prevent regression."), ("C", "Apply the hotfix only to the feature branch, assuming it will naturally reach production once the feature ships."), ("D", "Wait until the feature branch is complete and ship the hotfix bundled with the full feature release.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Branching the hotfix from the production tag isolates it from incomplete feature work, allowing an independent fast-tracked fix, and merging it back into all active branches afterward prevents the next regular release from silently reverting the fix. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Merging the entire incomplete feature branch into production to access hotfix code would release unfinished, untested functionality to end users.\n• C: Applying the fix only to the feature branch means production remains broken until that unrelated feature ships, which could be weeks away.\n• D: Bundling a critical fix with an unrelated feature release delays resolution of a production-impacting issue for no technical reason."),
        Question(id: "39", question: "Building — An admin makes several Flow and Page Layout changes directly in a sandbox as part of routine work, but forgets to retrieve them into source control before the sandbox is refreshed the following week, losing the changes. What process gap does this reveal, and what is the best fix?", options: [("A", "Ban admins from making any declarative changes in sandboxes going forward."), ("B", "Establish a mandatory 'retrieve and commit' step as part of every admin's daily workflow (or adopt source-tracked sandboxes/scratch orgs that automate change detection) so declarative changes are captured before any refresh."), ("C", "Extend the sandbox refresh interval to once a year so this happens less often."), ("D", "Require the admin to manually recreate the exact same Flow and Layout changes from memory after every refresh.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A disciplined retrieve-and-commit habit, or source-tracked environments that detect changes automatically, directly closes the gap between declarative work happening in a sandbox and it being captured in the permanent source-of-truth repository. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Banning declarative changes in sandboxes is impractical since admins routinely need to build and test Flows and layouts there.\n• C: A longer refresh interval reduces frequency of loss but doesn't address the underlying process gap, and delays legitimate refresh needs for other reasons.\n• D: Relying on memory to recreate lost configuration work is unreliable and not a real process fix."),
        Question(id: "40", question: "Building — Two developers each modify the same Profile XML file in separate feature branches, and merging causes a large, unreadable XML conflict that takes hours to resolve manually. What architectural change would most reduce this recurring problem going forward?", options: [("A", "Have both developers always work on the same feature branch to avoid any possibility of conflicts."), ("B", "Shift permission management from Profiles to Permission Sets where possible, reducing the size and conflict surface of Profile XML files, and keep feature branches short-lived to limit divergence."), ("C", "Store all Profile changes as screenshots instead of XML so Git cannot flag conflicts."), ("D", "Disable all permission changes for the remainder of the project.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Moving permission logic to Permission Sets directly shrinks Profile XML size and the resulting conflict surface, while short-lived branches limit how much divergence accumulates before a merge — both are established mitigations for this exact metadata conflict pattern. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Forcing both developers onto one branch defeats the purpose of feature isolation and doesn't scale as team size grows.\n• C: Screenshots aren't deployable metadata and would break the actual deployment pipeline entirely; Git also cannot version or diff images meaningfully for this purpose.\n• D: Disabling all permission changes halts legitimate development work rather than addressing the metadata conflict pattern."),
        Question(id: "41", question: "Building — After a production incident, the team wants a reliable way to identify the exact previous stable release to roll back to, and to correlate that release with its CAB approval record. What practice should the architect ensure is in place?", options: [("A", "Rely on commit timestamps alone, since they are always sufficient to identify release boundaries."), ("B", "Apply Git tags using semantic versioning at every production deployment, scripted into the CI/CD pipeline so tagging is never skipped, providing a clear rollback point and audit correlation to CAB approval records."), ("C", "Keep no tags at all and instead ask developers to recall which commit was deployed from memory."), ("D", "Tag only major releases, skipping minor and patch releases entirely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Automated semantic version tagging at every production deployment gives a precise, unambiguous rollback point and a natural anchor to correlate with change records and CAB approvals — exactly what's needed for reliable incident recovery. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Commit timestamps alone don't indicate which commits constituted an actual deployed release boundary, especially with multiple merges close together.\n• C: Relying on memory is unreliable and unauditable, especially under the pressure of an active incident.\n• D: Skipping minor/patch tags leaves no precise rollback point for the majority of releases, which are typically minor or patch level."),
        Question(id: "42", question: "Building — A team's Apex trigger passes all its unit tests, but silently fails in production during a bulk data load of 500 records, hitting a governor limit. Code review revealed the tests only ever inserted a single record. What testing practice gap caused this to go undetected?", options: [("A", "The team should have added more single-record tests with different field values."), ("B", "The team failed to include a bulk unit test that inserts 200+ records in a single DML operation to verify the trigger's bulkified behavior under governor limits."), ("C", "The team should have disabled the trigger in production to avoid the failure."), ("D", "The team should have run the single-record test more frequently in CI.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Triggers must handle bulk operations correctly; a bulk test with 200+ records in a single DML statement is the standard way to catch governor-limit and non-bulkified logic issues before they reach production. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Additional single-record tests with varied field values would still never exercise the bulk code path where the actual defect lives.\n• C: Disabling the trigger removes its business functionality entirely rather than fixing the underlying bulkification defect.\n• D: Running the same single-record test more often doesn't change what scenario it covers; frequency isn't the issue, scope is."),
        Question(id: "43", question: "Building — A team's Apex tests all pass in the sandbox where they were written, but the exact same tests fail intermittently when run in a different sandbox. Investigation shows several test classes are annotated with SeeAllData=true and depend on existing Opportunity records with specific stage values. What is the root cause and fix?", options: [("A", "The root cause is a Salesforce platform bug; file a support case and wait for a fix."), ("B", "The root cause is environment-dependent test data caused by SeeAllData=true; the fix is to remove SeeAllData=true wherever possible and create all needed test data within the test itself (or in @testSetup), reserving SeeAllData=true only for cases that strictly require it, like standard price books."), ("C", "The root cause is insufficient code coverage; add more assertions to the existing tests without changing their data dependency."), ("D", "The root cause is a missing index on the Opportunity object; ask an admin to add one.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: SeeAllData=true makes tests dependent on whatever real records happen to exist in a given org, which is exactly why the same test behaves differently across sandboxes; removing it in favor of self-contained test data eliminates that environment dependency. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: This is a well-documented anti-pattern in test design, not a platform defect, so a support case would not resolve it.\n• C: Adding assertions doesn't address the underlying data dependency causing environment-specific pass/fail behavior.\n• D: Missing indexes affect query performance, not the presence or absence of specific Opportunity records with certain stage values that the test depends on."),
        Question(id: "44", question: "Building — A team wants to verify that users without a specific permission set cannot view or edit a sensitive custom field, as part of their Apex test suite. Which testing approach correctly validates this?", options: [("A", "Query the field's metadata definition in a test and assert its type is Text."), ("B", "Wrap the test logic in System.runAs(testUserWithoutPermission) to execute the DML/query operations in that user's security context, and assert that FLS/sharing restrictions are correctly enforced."), ("C", "Run the test as the default test-running user (typically an admin) and assume permission enforcement is automatically validated."), ("D", "Manually log in as that user in production after deployment to visually check the page layout.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: System.runAs() executes code in a specified user's security context, enforcing sharing rules, FLS, and CRUD permissions, which is precisely the mechanism needed to test permission-based access restrictions in Apex. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Checking a field's declared data type says nothing about field-level security enforcement for a specific user's permissions.\n• C: The default test-running context typically runs with elevated System Administrator-like access, which would not surface a permission restriction that should apply to a lower-privilege user.\n• D: Manually checking in production after deployment is not an automated, repeatable unit test and defeats the purpose of having this validation caught before release."),
        Question(id: "45", question: "Building — A team building Apex that calls an external REST service needs to unit test the callout logic without making real HTTP requests during test execution (which Salesforce does not allow anyway). What is the correct approach?", options: [("A", "Skip testing the callout logic entirely and exclude that method from code coverage requirements."), ("B", "Implement a class that implements HttpCalloutMock, override its respond() method to return a predefined HttpResponse, and register it with Test.setMock(HttpCalloutMock.class, mockInstance) before invoking the code under test."), ("C", "Deploy the code without tests first, then add tests after confirming it works by manually calling the real endpoint from Execute Anonymous in production."), ("D", "Use SeeAllData=true so the test can make an actual outbound HTTP callout to the real service.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: HttpCalloutMock combined with Test.setMock() is the standard Apex testing pattern for deterministically simulating external HTTP responses, since real callouts are disallowed during test execution. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Excluding callout logic from coverage leaves a significant, often error-prone code path completely unverified.\n• C: Manually testing in production via Execute Anonymous is not a repeatable automated test and bypasses the standard unit test requirement entirely.\n• D: SeeAllData=true controls visibility to org data, not outbound HTTP behavior; Apex tests cannot make real callouts regardless of that annotation."),
        Question(id: "46", question: "Building — A customer currently deploys all customizations as one large monolithic set of metadata via Change Sets across a single sandbox path, and wants to move toward a more modular, scalable architecture as their team grows to 15 developers. Which development model transition should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Stay with org-based development and Change Sets indefinitely, since team size has no bearing on development model choice."), ("B", "Transition toward package-based development, likely starting with unlocked packages that decompose the org into bounded domains (e.g., Account, Billing), enabling independent, modular releases as the team scales."), ("C", "Immediately adopt fully namespaced managed packages, even though there is no plan to distribute externally."), ("D", "Switch every developer to work only in the production org directly, since that removes the need for packaging altogether.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: As team size and codebase complexity grow, package-based development with unlocked packages provides the modularity, independent release cadence, and source-driven structure needed to scale beyond what monolithic org-based Change Set deployments can support. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Team size and codebase complexity are exactly the factors that make monolithic Change Set deployment increasingly unmanageable, contradicting this option's claim.\n• C: Managed packages require a namespace and are designed for external ISV distribution with IP protection; adopting one for purely internal modularity adds unnecessary constraints and irreversible namespace commitment.\n• D: Working directly in production removes all pre-production safety and testing, and has nothing to do with modularity or packaging."),
        Question(id: "47", question: "Building — A developer's scratch org is set to expire in 3 days, but their feature branch work is not yet complete and would take another two weeks. What is the correct way to avoid losing their work?", options: [("A", "Do nothing; scratch orgs automatically extend themselves indefinitely if work is still incomplete."), ("B", "Ensure all changes are committed and pushed to source control regularly (the permanent record), and either extend the scratch org's duration (up to the 30-day maximum) or recreate a new scratch org from source control and re-seed it when needed."), ("C", "Copy the entire scratch org database to a local spreadsheet as a backup before it expires."), ("D", "Convert the scratch org into a Developer sandbox to avoid the expiration policy.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Since scratch orgs are inherently temporary (max 30 days) and all work is lost at expiration, the safe pattern is treating source control as the permanent record via frequent commits, and extending or recreating the scratch org as needed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Scratch orgs do not auto-extend; they expire strictly according to their configured duration regardless of whether work is finished.\n• C: Metadata and configuration in a scratch org cannot be meaningfully captured as a spreadsheet backup; this isn't how Salesforce metadata is structured or restorable.\n• D: There is no supported conversion path from a scratch org to a Developer sandbox; they are fundamentally different environment types."),
        Question(id: "48", question: "Building — A code review checklist currently only checks for compiling code and passing tests. After several incidents involving SOQL queries inside loops causing governor limit failures in production, the architect wants to strengthen the review process. What should be added, and how should it be enforced?", options: [("A", "Add a note to the team wiki asking developers to remember not to put SOQL in loops, with no automated enforcement."), ("B", "Integrate a static analysis tool such as PMD or Salesforce Code Analyzer into the CI pipeline with a rule that flags SOQL-in-loop and DML-in-loop patterns, configured to fail the build on such violations introduced in a pull request."), ("C", "Require every pull request to be reviewed by the entire engineering organization before merging."), ("D", "Remove code review from the process entirely and rely solely on production monitoring to catch these issues after deployment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Automated static analysis rules for SOQL/DML-in-loop patterns catch this exact defect class consistently and objectively in CI, before code reaches production, which is far more reliable than relying on memory during manual review. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A wiki note with no automated enforcement relies purely on individual developers remembering a rule, which is exactly the failure mode that caused the incidents.\n• C: Requiring the entire organization to review every PR doesn't specifically target this defect pattern and creates an unsustainable review bottleneck.\n• D: Removing code review and relying only on production monitoring means the defect reaches production before being caught, which is strictly worse than a pre-merge gate."),
        Question(id: "49", question: "Building — A team introduces a hard static analysis gate that fails any build with a single PMD violation, on a 5-year-old codebase with over 800 existing violations. Within a week, all pull requests are blocked and developers are bypassing the pipeline entirely to ship code. What went wrong, and what is the better approach?", options: [("A", "Nothing went wrong; the team should simply fix all 800 violations before doing any other work."), ("B", "The hard gate should have used a 'ratchet' approach — failing builds only on new violations introduced in the pull request's diff, while reporting (not blocking on) the pre-existing violations, allowing the team to progressively pay down debt without halting delivery."), ("C", "The static analysis tool should be permanently removed from the pipeline since it caused this disruption."), ("D", "The severity threshold should be raised so no violations are ever flagged, regardless of type.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A ratchet approach — gating only on newly introduced violations — lets a legacy codebase adopt static analysis without an impossible all-or-nothing remediation blocking all delivery, which is the standard pattern for introducing hard gates into brownfield code. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Demanding all 800 violations be fixed before any other work proceeds is unrealistic and exactly the all-or-nothing trap that caused developers to bypass the pipeline.\n• C: Removing static analysis entirely throws away real code quality value instead of fixing the rollout approach that caused the disruption.\n• D: Setting the threshold so nothing is ever flagged defeats the entire purpose of static analysis and leaves no quality gate at all."),
        Question(id: "50", question: "Building — A pull request author and the person who deploys that same change to production are, per current process, frequently the same individual, with no independent review. After a compliance audit flags this, what change should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Continue the current process but add a note in the ticket saying the author reviewed their own work."), ("B", "Require a minimum of one independent peer reviewer approval on every pull request (with a tech lead review for major changes), and ensure the person deploying to production is not the sole author of the change, to satisfy segregation of duties."), ("C", "Eliminate the pull request process entirely to speed up delivery."), ("D", "Allow the author to approve their own pull request as long as they wait 24 hours before merging.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Segregation of duties requires that the person authoring a change is not the sole approver or deployer of that same change; mandatory independent peer review directly satisfies this compliance requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A self-review note does not constitute independent review and would not satisfy a segregation-of-duties compliance requirement.\n• C: Eliminating PR review entirely removes any quality or compliance gate rather than fixing the self-approval problem.\n• D: A time delay does not introduce an independent reviewer; the same individual is still both author and sole approver, which is the exact issue being audited.\n\n# Deploying"),
        Question(id: "51", question: "Deploying — A team needs to deploy 8,500 metadata components in a single release, including new custom objects, fields, Apex classes, and Lightning components, to a connected sandbox that is not the org they originally developed in. Change Sets aren't an option since it isn't a directly connected org. What deployment approach should they use?", options: [("A", "Manually recreate every component by hand in the target org through Setup."), ("B", "Use Metadata API (via Salesforce CLI) to deploy the package to the target org, since it supports deployment to any connected org (not just point-to-point) and can handle large component counts up to the API's per-deployment file limit."), ("C", "Use the ANT Migration Tool exclusively, since Metadata API cannot handle more than 100 components per deployment."), ("D", "Split the deployment into ten separate Change Sets and deploy them sequentially between the same two orgs over several days.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Metadata API (accessed via CLI) can deploy to any connected org regardless of a pre-established Change Set connection, and supports large deployments (up to 10,000 files per deploy), making it the right tool for this scenario. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Manually recreating 8,500 components is operationally infeasible and defeats the purpose of having deployment tooling at all.\n• C: The claim that Metadata API is limited to 100 components is false; it supports far larger deployments, and ANT is a legacy tool with no advantage here.\n• D: Change Sets require a point-to-point deployment connection between the specific orgs involved, which the scenario states doesn't exist here; splitting into multiple Change Sets doesn't solve that missing connection."),
        Question(id: "52", question: "Deploying — A release includes a change to a Connected App's OAuth configuration and a new Custom Setting with default values that integrations depend on. The team assumes their standard Metadata API deployment will fully handle both. What should the architect flag?", options: [("A", "Both items deploy completely automatically with no manual steps required."), ("B", "The Connected App metadata type is deployable but its OAuth consumer key/secret must be reconfigured manually in the target org; and while the Custom Setting object type is deployable, the actual data/default values held in Custom Settings records typically require a separate step (Data Loader, CLI data commands, or Hierarchy default values) beyond the base metadata deploy."), ("C", "Custom Settings can never be deployed by any means and must always be entered manually field by field with no automation option."), ("D", "Connected Apps cannot be included in any Metadata API deployment under any circumstances.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: These are documented Metadata API limitations — OAuth secrets require manual reconfiguration per org for security reasons, and Custom Settings data (versus the object type itself) typically needs a separate data deployment step, both of which should be called out explicitly in the deployment runbook. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Assuming full automation for these two specific items risks a broken integration post-deployment since manual/separate steps are genuinely required.\n• C: Custom Settings data can be deployed via automated means like CLI data commands; claiming it can never be automated overstates the limitation.\n• D: Connected App metadata itself is deployable via Metadata API; only the OAuth secret requires manual reconfiguration, not the entire component."),
        Question(id: "53", question: "Deploying — A team needs to permanently remove two deprecated custom fields and one Apex class from production as part of an upcoming release, using Salesforce CLI. What is the correct mechanism, and what key risk should be flagged?", options: [("A", "Simply omit the components from the package.xml; Metadata API will automatically infer they should be deleted."), ("B", "Include a destructiveChanges.xml (or destructiveChangesPre.xml if deletion must precede additive changes) listing the components to delete, alongside the package.xml; flag that Salesforce has no native one-click rollback for destructive deployments, so a backup of the current metadata should be taken first."), ("C", "Use a Change Set, which fully supports combining destructive and additive changes in a single deployment."), ("D", "Ask end users to manually delete the fields and class themselves after the deployment completes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Destructive changes require an explicit destructiveChanges.xml (or Pre variant) in the deployment package; because there's no native rollback for deletions, taking a metadata backup beforehand is the standard risk mitigation. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Omitting a component from package.xml simply means it isn't touched by that deployment; Metadata API does not infer deletion intent from absence.\n• C: Change Sets explicitly do not support combining destructive and additive changes in the same deployment, unlike CLI-based Metadata API deployments.\n• D: Asking end users, who typically lack the necessary permissions and expertise, to manually delete metadata components is not a controlled or auditable release process."),
        Question(id: "54", question: "Deploying — A production deployment fails midway due to an unrelated validation error in a component nobody intended to change, and the team has no documented way to know what production looked like immediately beforehand. What deployment practice gap does this expose?", options: [("A", "The team should have deployed on a Friday afternoon to reduce user impact."), ("B", "The team lacked a pre-deployment backup of current production metadata (a 'rollback package' retrieved and stored in source control) and a validated rollback procedure, both of which should be standard pre-deployment checklist items."), ("C", "The team should stop using Metadata API entirely and switch exclusively to manual Setup changes."), ("D", "The team should have skipped running any tests to save time before this deployment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Retrieving and storing a pre-deployment metadata snapshot, and having a validated rollback procedure, is the standard mitigation for exactly this situation — an unexpected mid-deployment failure with no clear path back to the prior known-good state. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Deployment timing doesn't address the missing backup and rollback procedure, which is the actual structural gap.\n• C: Abandoning Metadata API in favor of manual Setup changes removes automation and traceability entirely, making the situation worse, not better.\n• D: Skipping tests would increase risk further and has no relationship to having a documented rollback path."),
        Question(id: "55", question: "Deploying — Choose 2. A team plans a production deployment during a defined maintenance window and needs to prevent user-triggered automation from firing on records created or modified as part of the deployment or related data scripts. Which two pre-deployment steps should be included in the runbook to mitigate this? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Deactivate the specific Flows, Process Builders, or workflow rules that could trigger unintended automation during the deployment window, with a plan to reactivate them post-deployment."), ("B", "Pause any scheduled batch jobs that could run and interact with data during the deployment window."), ("C", "Permanently delete all automation in the org so it can never interfere with any future deployment."), ("D", "Grant every user temporary System Administrator access during the deployment window."), ("E", "Change all users' passwords immediately before the deployment begins.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Temporarily deactivating relevant automation and pausing scheduled jobs during the deployment window are standard, reversible pre-deployment steps that prevent unintended automation firing on in-flight changes without permanently altering the org's configuration. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Permanently deleting automation removes legitimate business logic the org depends on well beyond the deployment window, which is a drastically disproportionate and irreversible step.\n• D: Granting every user admin access during a deployment window is a serious security risk and has no relationship to preventing automation from firing.\n• E: Forcing a password change has no effect on whether Flows, Process Builders, or workflow rules fire during the deployment."),
        Question(id: "56", question: "Deploying — After a production deployment completes successfully, several new permission sets exist in the org but no users have access to the features they were built to expose, and a new record type isn't showing up on any page layout assignment. What deployment step was likely missed?", options: [("A", "The Metadata API deployment itself failed silently and needs to be re-run from scratch."), ("B", "The post-deployment steps — assigning the new permission sets to appropriate users/public groups and updating page layout assignments for the new record type — were not executed, since these actions are typically not automated by the metadata deployment itself."), ("C", "The org has reached its permission set license limit, which is why nothing appears."), ("D", "Custom Metadata Types need to be manually re-enabled after every deployment for any new features to appear.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Permission set assignment to users/groups and layout assignment for new record types are common post-deployment activities that Metadata API deployment does not automatically perform; they must be executed as explicit post-deploy steps in the runbook. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: The scenario states the deployment completed successfully; a silent full failure would typically surface as missing metadata entirely, not just missing assignments.\n• C: A permission set license limit would produce an explicit licensing error, not simply an absence of any user assignment.\n• D: Custom Metadata Types have no bearing on permission set assignment or record type layout visibility; this is an invented mechanism."),
        Question(id: "57", question: "Deploying — A release introduces a new required field on the Account object that must be populated for all 2 million existing Account records before the dependent Apex validation goes live, or the validation will block every subsequent Account update. What is the best approach to handle this?", options: [("A", "Deploy the validation rule first and let users manually populate the field on each Account as they happen to edit it."), ("B", "Run a backfill data migration (via Batch Apex or a bulk data tool) to populate the new field on all existing records as a scheduled pre- or post-deployment step, validated first in a staging sandbox, before or alongside activating the dependent validation."), ("C", "Apply the required field and validation rule only to newly created Accounts going forward, ignoring the 2 million existing records entirely."), ("D", "Ask each of the org's business users to manually export and re-import their own subset of Account records with the field populated.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A scheduled batch backfill, tested first in staging, ensures the new required field is populated across all existing records before the dependent validation is enforced, avoiding a mass lockout of Account updates. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Deploying the validation before the backfill means any user editing an Account without the new field populated would be immediately blocked, causing widespread disruption.\n• C: Ignoring existing records leaves 2 million Accounts permanently unable to satisfy the new business rule, and validation logic often needs to apply org-wide, not just to new records.\n• D: Relying on individual business users to manually export/import data at this scale is unrealistic, error-prone, and not a controlled data migration process."),
        Question(id: "58", question: "Deploying — Choose 2. A deployment package includes a new Flow that references a custom field, and an Apex class that calls that Flow. The team deploys everything as a single package.xml with no destructive changes involved. Which two statements about dependency handling are accurate for this deployment? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Deploying all interdependent components (the custom field, Flow, and Apex class) together in a single package deployment lets Metadata API resolve their interdependencies without requiring manual ordering."), ("B", "If the custom field the Flow references doesn't already exist in the target org and isn't included in this same deployment, the deployment will fail due to the missing dependency."), ("C", "Metadata API deployments never fail due to missing dependencies as long as test coverage is above 75%."), ("D", "Flows and Apex are deployed via entirely separate APIs that cannot be combined in the same deployment transaction."), ("E", "Destructive changes must always be included in every deployment package regardless of whether anything is being deleted.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Bundling interdependent components together lets the platform resolve their dependencies as part of one atomic deployment, and a genuinely missing dependency (the field neither existing nor included) will cause deployment failure regardless of test coverage. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Code coverage percentage is unrelated to whether component dependencies (like a missing custom field) resolve correctly; coverage is a separate deployment gate.\n• D: Flows and Apex classes are both deployable via Metadata API and can be included in the very same deployment package and transaction.\n• E: Destructive changes files are only needed when components are actually being deleted; they are not a mandatory part of every deployment."),
        Question(id: "59", question: "Deploying — A team wants to reduce production deployment downtime for a release that was already validated successfully two days ago with all tests passing at 82% coverage. No metadata has changed in the target org since that validation. What deployment option should they use?", options: [("A", "Re-run a full deployment with RunAllTests to be extra safe, even though this significantly increases deployment time."), ("B", "Use Quick Deploy to deploy the already-validated changes without re-running tests, since the validation succeeded within the last 10 days, coverage requirements were already met, and no changes have been made to the validated metadata since."), ("C", "Deploy with NoTestRun, since the Apex classes were already tested two days ago and don't need to be tested again in this specific deployment."), ("D", "Wait an additional 8 days so the validation is closer to its expiration before deploying.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Quick Deploy is designed exactly for this scenario — a successful validation within the 10-day window, met coverage thresholds, and no subsequent metadata changes — allowing deployment without re-running tests and significantly reducing downtime. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Re-running RunAllTests when Quick Deploy is available defeats the entire purpose of validating in advance and unnecessarily extends the deployment window.\n• C: NoTestRun is rejected by Salesforce for any production deployment that includes Apex; it is not a valid way to skip tests based on a prior test run.\n• D: Waiting longer only risks exceeding the 10-day Quick Deploy eligibility window, increasing risk rather than reducing it."),
        Question(id: "60", question: "Deploying — A customer wants to store integration endpoint URLs and feature toggle values in a way that can be included directly in their deployment package and version-controlled like other metadata, since these values must be consistent and auditable across dev, QA, and staging. Which storage mechanism should the architect recommend, and why?", options: [("A", "Custom Settings, since their records are metadata and are automatically included in every Metadata API deployment package alongside the object type."), ("B", "Custom Metadata Types, since their records are themselves metadata (not object data), can be queried in SOQL, and can be included directly in a deployment package and version-controlled, unlike Custom Setting record data."), ("C", "Standard picklist values on a custom field, since picklists are inherently deployable and versioned."), ("D", "A spreadsheet maintained outside Salesforce and manually re-entered into each environment after every deployment.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Custom Metadata Type records are metadata, not object data, meaning they deploy with the package and are naturally version-controlled and consistent across environments, exactly matching the stated requirement. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Custom Settings records are object data, not metadata; only the Custom Setting's structure (the object type) deploys automatically, not the actual field values within its records.\n• C: Picklist values represent a fixed list of options for a field, not structured configuration records with multiple attributes like endpoint URLs; this doesn't fit the described need.\n• D: A manually maintained external spreadsheet reintroduces the exact consistency and auditability problem the customer is trying to solve by moving away from manual re-entry."),
        Question(id: "61", question: "Deploying — A support team needs a specific integration endpoint URL to differ between the Staging and Production orgs, while the underlying Apex logic that queries it stays identical across both. Using Custom Metadata Types, what is the correct approach?", options: [("A", "Hardcode both URLs directly in the Apex class using an if/else statement checking the org's OrgId, then redeploy the class whenever a URL changes."), ("B", "Create Custom Metadata Type records with an environment identifier field (or separate per-environment records), and have Apex query the correct record deterministically at runtime; deploy the appropriate record(s) to each environment as part of the release, or manage them independently per org outside the deployed package."), ("C", "Store both URLs as hardcoded string literals inside a single Apex trigger with no metadata record involved."), ("D", "Manually type the URL into the Setup UI in a random location in Production every time it changes, without touching any deployment artifact.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Using CMT records with an environment identifier (or per-environment records managed separately) lets identical Apex logic query the correct configuration value deterministically per org, cleanly separating environment-specific configuration from code logic. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Hardcoding URLs in an if/else statement means any URL change requires a full class redeployment, defeating the purpose of externalized configuration.\n• C: Storing configuration values as string literals in a trigger mixes configuration with code and requires redeployment for any value change, the opposite of a clean CMT-based approach.\n• D: Manually entering values with no deployment artifact or tracking mechanism reintroduces the same auditability and consistency problems CMT is meant to solve."),
        Question(id: "62", question: "Deploying — A customer's release includes updated Report Type metadata and several new Dashboards, along with a request to have those dashboards automatically emailed to a specific distribution list every Monday. The team assumes the entire configuration, including the schedule, will deploy via Metadata API. What should the architect flag?", options: [("A", "Report Type metadata and Dashboard XML are generally deployable via Metadata API, but dashboard running-user settings and scheduled subscription/email delivery configuration are typically not part of the deployable metadata and must be configured manually in the target org after deployment."), ("B", "Reports and Dashboards cannot be deployed via Metadata API under any circumstances and must always be manually recreated from scratch in each org."), ("C", "Scheduled dashboard email delivery is fully included in the Metadata API package and requires no manual configuration whatsoever."), ("D", "Reports and Dashboards deploy automatically only if the org has Report Builder enabled as a separate paid add-on.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: While the structural metadata (report types, dashboard XML) is deployable, operational settings like the dashboard's running user and scheduled email subscriptions are execution-time configuration that typically requires manual setup after deployment, and this should be explicitly documented in the runbook. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• B: Report Type metadata and Dashboard definitions are, in fact, deployable via Metadata API; claiming they can never be deployed is incorrect.\n• C: Scheduled email subscriptions are specifically one of the manual, non-deployable configuration items that must be set up after deployment, not something the package handles automatically.\n• D: There is no such paid add-on requirement gating whether report/dashboard metadata deploys; this is a fabricated constraint."),
        Question(id: "63", question: "Deploying — A CI pipeline is configured to deploy to production using RunSpecifiedTests, listing only the five test classes the developer believes are relevant to their change. The deployment succeeds, but a production incident later reveals an untested code path in an unrelated class was broken by a shared utility change. What deployment testing gap does this reveal?", options: [("A", "RunSpecifiedTests guarantees org-wide 75% coverage automatically regardless of which tests are listed, so this could not have been prevented."), ("B", "RunSpecifiedTests only validates 75% coverage for the specific classes being deployed based on the specified tests, not org-wide impact; when a shared/utility component changes, the team should ensure tests covering all its dependents are included, or use RunLocalTests for changes to broadly shared components."), ("C", "The incident was purely a platform bug unrelated to test selection strategy."), ("D", "NoTestRun should have been used instead, since it provides broader test coverage than RunSpecifiedTests.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: RunSpecifiedTests coverage requirements apply narrowly to the deployed components covered by the listed tests, not the whole org; changes to widely shared/utility code require broader test selection (or RunLocalTests) to catch downstream impacts across dependent classes. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: RunSpecifiedTests does not guarantee org-wide coverage; it only confirms 75% coverage for the specific deployed components based on the tests actually run.\n• C: This is a direct consequence of test selection scope, not an unrelated platform defect.\n• D: NoTestRun skips test execution entirely for Apex-inclusive deployments to production and would be rejected by Salesforce; it does not provide broader coverage than RunSpecifiedTests."),
        Question(id: "64", question: "Deploying — Choose 2. An architect is reviewing a customer's approach to managing environment-specific reference data (e.g., differing tax rate tables between a UAT and Production org) that Apex code depends on at runtime. Which two approaches are valid, given the trade-offs between deployability and flexibility? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Store the values as Custom Metadata Type records included directly in the deployed package, giving strong deployability and version control at the cost of needing a new package version for every value change."), ("B", "Store the values in Custom Settings and use Salesforce CLI data commands or a scripted post-deploy step to set the correct environment-specific values after each deployment, trading some deployment automation for more flexible, code-free updates."), ("C", "Hardcode both the UAT and Production tax rate values directly inside a shared Apex trigger with no conditional logic distinguishing environments."), ("D", "Store the values only in a developer's personal notes app, to be manually re-entered from memory whenever asked."), ("E", "Disable all tax calculation logic in UAT permanently so the values never need to be managed there at all.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Both CMT-in-package and Custom-Settings-with-scripted-post-deploy-values are legitimate, documented patterns for managing environment-specific reference data, each with a real trade-off between strict deployability/versioning and flexible, code-free updates. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Hardcoding both environments' values with no conditional logic to distinguish them means the wrong value could apply in either org, and any change requires a full class redeploy.\n• D: Personal notes with no system of record provide no auditability, consistency, or deployability whatsoever.\n• E: Disabling tax logic in UAT prevents any meaningful testing of tax-related functionality before it reaches production, undermining the purpose of the UAT environment.\n\n# Testing"),
        Question(id: "65", question: "Testing — A customer's current test approach is almost entirely manual UI testing performed by a QA team over a full week before every release, with very few Apex unit tests. Releases have slowed as functionality has grown. What testing methodology shift should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Hire more manual QA testers to cover the growing functionality faster."), ("B", "Rebalance toward the testing pyramid: invest heavily in fast, developer-owned Apex unit tests as the broad base, add focused integration tests for cross-component behavior, and reserve manual/UI testing for a smaller set of high-value end-to-end and exploratory scenarios."), ("C", "Eliminate all manual testing immediately and rely solely on UI automation for every test case, including trivial unit-level logic."), ("D", "Extend the QA week to two weeks to allow more thorough manual coverage without changing the test mix.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Rebalancing toward the testing pyramid — more cheap, fast unit tests at the base and fewer expensive manual/UI tests at the top — reduces cycle time while improving defect detection, which directly addresses the slowing release cadence. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Adding more manual testers scales cost linearly with functionality growth without addressing the inverted, slow-by-design pyramid shape causing the bottleneck.\n• C: Replacing all manual testing with UI automation, including trivial unit-level logic, misapplies UI automation to cases better and more cheaply covered by fast unit tests.\n• D: Extending the QA week without changing the test mix just accepts a slower cadence rather than fixing the structural inefficiency."),
        Question(id: "66", question: "Testing — A team currently only runs static analysis and Apex tests in a nightly batch job, separate from the CI pipeline, meaning defects are discovered up to 24 hours after being introduced. What is the recommended shift-left change, and why?", options: [("A", "Reduce the nightly batch job frequency to weekly to reduce noise from frequent failures."), ("B", "Move static analysis and Apex test execution into the CI pipeline so they run on every pull request, catching defects at commit time rather than up to a day later, since earlier detection is significantly cheaper to fix."), ("C", "Keep the nightly batch job as-is, since defect discovery timing has no cost impact regardless of when it happens."), ("D", "Move static analysis and test execution entirely into UAT, so business users can discover code-level defects during acceptance testing.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Shift-left testing means moving quality checks as early as possible in the pipeline; running static analysis and tests on every PR catches issues at commit time, which is dramatically cheaper to fix than after a day-long delay or in later stages. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Reducing frequency to weekly increases, not decreases, the average time between defect introduction and discovery, the opposite of shift-left.\n• C: Defect discovery timing has a well-documented cost multiplier (the later a bug is found, the more expensive it is to fix), so timing absolutely matters.\n• D: Pushing static analysis and unit-level test execution into UAT moves code-level quality checks to the wrong audience and the wrong (very late) stage of the pipeline."),
        Question(id: "67", question: "Testing — A complex org with heavy Flow-based automation and multiple external integrations wants a testing methodology that covers this landscape appropriately. Apex unit tests alone aren't verifying the Flow logic or integration behavior. What should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Rely exclusively on Apex unit tests and skip any dedicated verification of Flow logic or integration endpoints, since Apex coverage requirements are the only mandatory testing gate."), ("B", "Combine Apex unit and integration tests for code-level logic, add end-to-end automated tests (e.g., Provar or Copado Robotic Testing) for critical cross-object business processes touching Flows, and use callout mocking to isolate integration endpoint testing, supplemented by targeted manual exploratory testing for edge cases."), ("C", "Manually test every possible combination of Flow branches and integration responses by hand before every release, with no automation at all."), ("D", "Disable all Flows during testing since they cannot be tested reliably, and re-enable them only after deployment to production.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A layered approach — Apex tests for code logic, end-to-end automation for cross-object/Flow-driven processes, mocked callouts for integration isolation, and targeted manual testing for edge cases — covers the full breadth of a complex, Flow- and integration-heavy org that Apex tests alone cannot verify. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Apex coverage requirements only measure Apex code; they say nothing about whether Flow logic or integration behavior actually works correctly.\n• C: Manually testing every Flow branch and integration response combination by hand doesn't scale and isn't sustainable release over release.\n• D: Disabling Flows during testing means the automation that drives major business processes is never actually verified before reaching production."),
        Question(id: "68", question: "Testing — A team releases weekly and has accumulated a 6-hour automated regression suite that is now delaying every release window. Some tests cover low-risk configuration items like email template formatting that rarely change or break. What should the architect recommend to right-size the suite?", options: [("A", "Keep every existing test forever regardless of value, since removing any test always increases risk."), ("B", "Review and prune the regression suite periodically: keep automated coverage focused on high-value, stable, frequently-exercised core business processes and critical Apex logic, and deprioritize or remove low-risk, rarely-changing configuration checks like email template formatting, aiming to keep the suite fast enough to fit the release cadence."), ("C", "Double the size of the QA team so they can run the full 6-hour suite manually alongside automation every week."), ("D", "Run the regression suite only once per quarter regardless of weekly release cadence.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Periodic suite review that focuses automation on high-value, high-risk areas and prunes low-value, rarely-changing checks keeps the suite fast and relevant to actual risk, which is the standard fix for a bloated regression suite outpacing release cadence. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Keeping every test forever regardless of value is exactly what caused the suite to bloat to 6 hours in the first place; not all tests carry equal risk-reduction value.\n• C: Adding manual testers to run the same tests in parallel adds cost without addressing the suite's underlying inefficiency or its mismatch with release cadence.\n• D: Running regression only quarterly against a weekly release cadence leaves most weekly releases essentially unregression-tested, which increases risk."),
        Question(id: "69", question: "Testing — A team wants to introduce UI automation for Salesforce Lightning but has no prior experience and a limited budget. Their manual regression of the 5 most business-critical end-to-end flows currently takes about 3 hours per weekly release. What is the most appropriate starting approach?", options: [("A", "Attempt to automate 100% of all UI test cases, including every minor edge case, in the first sprint using Selenium built entirely from scratch."), ("B", "Start with automating the 5-10 highest-value, most business-critical end-to-end scenarios first (using a Salesforce-purpose-built tool suited to their budget and skill level), rather than trying to automate everything immediately."), ("C", "Skip UI automation entirely forever, since 3 hours of manual regression per week is considered acceptable at any team size or release frequency."), ("D", "Only automate low-value, rarely-used test cases first, saving the business-critical scenarios for manual testing indefinitely.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Starting with a small set of high-value, high-risk end-to-end scenarios lets a team new to UI automation build confidence and see quick ROI, rather than attempting comprehensive automation immediately, which commonly fails for inexperienced teams. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Attempting to automate every scenario immediately, especially with no prior experience, sets an unrealistic scope that typically stalls or fails outright.\n• C: Whether 3 hours is 'acceptable' depends on team capacity and growth trajectory; dismissing automation entirely ignores the value of reducing recurring manual effort over time.\n• D: Automating low-value cases first while leaving critical scenarios manual inverts the priority; the highest-risk flows should be automated first for the best return."),
        Question(id: "70", question: "Testing — An org-wide Apex coverage report shows 78% coverage, comfortably above the 75% deployment threshold. A developer is about to deploy a brand-new class with 0% coverage as part of an unrelated feature. What should the architect flag as the risk here, and is the deployment technically possible?", options: [("A", "The deployment is impossible; Salesforce blocks any deployment containing a class with 0% coverage regardless of org-wide totals."), ("B", "The deployment is technically possible as long as the resulting org-wide coverage (including the new 0%-covered class) remains at or above 75%; however, the architect should flag that a large 0%-covered class can be enough to drag the overall percentage below the threshold, and recommend adding real tests before deployment regardless of whether it's technically permitted."), ("C", "Org-wide coverage is recalculated to exclude any newly deployed classes for the first 30 days, so this has no impact on the coverage threshold."), ("D", "A class with 0% coverage automatically triggers a mandatory CAB review with no exceptions, regardless of org-wide totals.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Salesforce evaluates coverage org-wide, not per-class, so a new 0%-covered class can still deploy if the org-wide total stays at or above 75% — but this is a real risk since a large enough untested class can single-handedly drag the average below threshold on a future deployment. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Salesforce does not block deployment purely because a specific class has 0% coverage; it evaluates the org-wide aggregate against the 75% threshold.\n• C: There is no such 30-day grace period exclusion for newly deployed classes in Salesforce's coverage calculation.\n• D: There is no platform mechanism that automatically triggers a mandatory CAB review based on a class's coverage percentage; that would be a customer-defined governance policy, not a platform behavior."),
        Question(id: "71", question: "Testing — A CI pipeline currently uses RunAllTests for every feature branch validation, including managed package tests, and pipeline runs now take over an hour, frustrating developers waiting for feedback on small changes. What change should the architect recommend for the feature-branch validation stage specifically?", options: [("A", "Switch feature-branch CI validation to RunSpecifiedTests, scoped to the tests relevant to the changed components, to get fast targeted feedback, while reserving RunAllTests (or RunLocalTests) for less frequent, comprehensive regression runs or production deployment."), ("B", "Switch to NoTestRun for feature-branch validation to eliminate test execution time entirely, since tests will run eventually in production anyway."), ("C", "Keep RunAllTests for every single feature branch validation forever, since faster feedback isn't actually valuable to developers."), ("D", "Disable the CI pipeline for feature branches entirely and only run tests once a quarter.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: RunSpecifiedTests scoped to relevant tests gives fast, targeted feedback appropriate for frequent feature-branch validation, while comprehensive RunAllTests/RunLocalTests runs are better reserved for less frequent full-regression or production-bound validation, balancing speed and thoroughness. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• B: NoTestRun is rejected for any production deployment containing Apex, and skipping tests entirely in CI removes the fast feedback loop the pipeline exists to provide.\n• C: Fast feedback is a well-established value driver for developer productivity; dismissing it ignores the actual problem the team is experiencing.\n• D: Disabling CI validation for feature branches removes the ability to catch defects before merge entirely, which is a much bigger risk than a slow pipeline."),
        Question(id: "72", question: "Testing — Choose 2. A package-based development team has several unlocked packages, each maintaining its own Apex test classes. During a production deployment via RunLocalTests, the team is surprised the org-wide coverage doesn't reflect certain managed package Apex they rely on for a shared integration utility. Which two statements accurately explain this behavior and the correct response? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "RunLocalTests explicitly excludes managed package tests from the coverage calculation, which is why that Apex isn't reflected; if managed package Apex meaningfully contributes to relied-upon logic, the team should use RunAllTests when that dependency's coverage needs to be included."), ("B", "Each unlocked package independently needs to maintain its own 75% coverage for its own Apex components; package-scoped CI runs (e.g., 'sf apex run test' scoped to a package directory) help validate this at the package level during development."), ("C", "Managed package Apex is always automatically counted in org-wide coverage under every test run option with no exceptions."), ("D", "Unlocked packages are exempt from the org-wide 75% coverage requirement entirely, regardless of what RunLocalTests or RunAllTests is used."), ("E", "Coverage calculations are always identical between RunLocalTests and RunAllTests, since both execute the exact same set of test classes.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: RunLocalTests specifically omits managed package tests from the coverage calculation (explaining the surprise), and each unlocked package must independently satisfy 75% coverage for its own Apex, which package-scoped CI test runs help verify during development. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Managed package Apex coverage is explicitly excluded under RunLocalTests, contradicting the claim that it's always counted under every test run option.\n• D: Unlocked packages are not exempt from the 75% coverage requirement; their own Apex components must independently meet it.\n• E: RunLocalTests and RunAllTests differ specifically in whether they include managed package tests, so their resulting test sets and coverage calculations are not identical."),
        Question(id: "73", question: "Testing — A QA sandbox is refreshed as a Partial Copy sandbox containing a 10,000-record sample of production Accounts, but testers report the sample doesn't include enough of the specific record types and edge-case data combinations they need to validate a new feature. What is the best way to address this without resorting to a Full Copy sandbox?", options: [("A", "Give up on using the Partial Copy sandbox and always test exclusively in production going forward."), ("B", "Adjust the Partial Copy sandbox template's object criteria/filters to better target the specific record types and data combinations needed, and supplement with a test data factory or scripted seeding of additional edge-case records specifically for the new feature."), ("C", "Manually create every single edge-case record by hand in the UI each time a new sandbox refresh occurs, with no scripting or reusable process."), ("D", "Convert the QA sandbox into a Developer sandbox, which will automatically contain more representative production data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Refining the Partial Copy template's sampling criteria targets more relevant production-like data, and supplementing with scripted seeding of specific edge cases directly closes the gap without the cost and long refresh cycle of a Full Copy sandbox. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Testing exclusively in production abandons any pre-production safety net and risks impacting real customers and data.\n• C: Manual, unscripted recreation of edge cases after every refresh is not sustainable and is exactly the kind of repetitive manual work automation is meant to eliminate.\n• D: Developer sandboxes contain no production data at all by default; converting to one would move in the opposite direction of having representative data."),
        Question(id: "74", question: "Testing — A customer wants to give developers realistic-looking test data in lower sandboxes without ever exposing real customer PII, and currently exports raw Full Copy sandbox data directly into Developer sandboxes with no changes. What should the architect recommend, and at what point in the process?", options: [("A", "Apply data masking/anonymization (e.g., via Salesforce Data Mask or a custom batch process) to transform PII into realistic but fake data before it is placed into any non-production environment accessible to a broader developer population, not after."), ("B", "Continue exporting raw production data unchanged, since Developer sandboxes are considered fully secure environments with no compliance exposure."), ("C", "Mask the data only after developers have already had access to it for a few weeks, since masking timing doesn't materially affect risk."), ("D", "Encrypt the exported CSV file with a password but keep the actual field values as real, unmasked production data.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [0], explanation: "Why correct: Masking or anonymizing PII before it lands in any environment accessible to a broader (often less-trusted) developer population is the standard control point; doing it before, not after, exposure is what actually prevents the leakage risk. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• B: Developer sandboxes are not exempt from data privacy obligations just because they're non-production; real PII there still carries compliance risk.\n• C: Masking after weeks of exposure means the PII was already accessible to developers during that window, which is exactly the risk masking is meant to prevent.\n• D: Password-encrypting a file containing unmasked values still exposes real PII to anyone who has (or obtains) the password; it doesn't substitute for actually anonymizing the field values."),
        Question(id: "75", question: "Testing — Six months after a major data model change, a team's regression test suite still creates test records using the old field structure via a test data factory that was never updated. Some tests silently pass despite bypassing new required validation logic that would apply to real user data. What process gap does this reveal?", options: [("A", "Test data factories, once written, never need to be revisited regardless of schema changes."), ("B", "Test data factory code should be stored in source control alongside application code and updated whenever the schema changes, and test data should be periodically reviewed against current business processes to catch drift like this before it produces false-passing tests."), ("C", "The correct fix is to disable the new required field validation instead of updating the aging test data factory."), ("D", "Regression tests should be deleted entirely rather than maintained going forward.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Keeping test data factories in source control alongside the schema they model, and periodically reviewing test data against current business processes, is exactly the maintenance discipline that prevents stale test data from silently masking real validation gaps. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Test data factories absolutely need to evolve alongside schema changes; treating them as permanently static is what caused this exact drift.\n• C: Disabling new required validation logic to accommodate outdated test data addresses the symptom in the wrong direction and removes a legitimate business rule.\n• D: Deleting the regression suite entirely removes all automated protection rather than fixing the specific maintenance gap that caused stale data."),
        Question(id: "76", question: "Testing — Choose 2. An architect is designing a unified test data strategy across a customer's Developer sandboxes, QA Partial Copy sandbox, and Staging Full Copy sandbox, with a requirement that no real PII ever appears in any environment below Staging. Which two elements should be included in the strategy? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Use masked/anonymized data derived from the Partial Copy sandbox (or a data classification-driven masking process) to seed Developer sandboxes, ensuring consistent, representative, but non-identifying data below Staging."), ("B", "Automate data seeding as part of the sandbox refresh pipeline so environments are reliably repopulated with the correct dataset after every refresh, rather than relying on manual re-entry."), ("C", "Populate Developer sandboxes directly from an unmasked Full Copy export, since Developer sandboxes are considered low-risk by default."), ("D", "Require every developer to manually request individual production records be emailed to them whenever test data is needed."), ("E", "Store all test data exclusively as hardcoded values inside page layouts.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Deriving masked, representative data for lower environments and automating its seeding as part of the refresh pipeline directly satisfies both the 'no PII below Staging' requirement and the need for consistent, repeatable environment setup. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Populating Developer sandboxes from an unmasked Full Copy export directly violates the stated requirement that no real PII appear below Staging.\n• D: Emailing individual production records to developers on request is an ungoverned, ad hoc data distribution channel that both violates the PII requirement and doesn't scale.\n• E: Page layouts are UI configuration, not a data storage mechanism, and cannot hold test records at all."),
        Question(id: "77", question: "Testing — A QA team reports that automated regression tests pass reliably in isolation but frequently fail when run in parallel with other test classes in the same CI job, due to shared static Apex variables and inconsistent record states across tests. What is the correct fix, and why does it matter for CI performance?", options: [("A", "Disable parallel test execution across the entire org permanently, accepting a much slower CI pipeline as an unavoidable trade-off."), ("B", "Refactor tests to avoid shared static state and ensure each test method creates and depends only on its own isolated data (via proper @testSetup or factory-created records scoped to that test), preserving the ability to run tests in parallel, which Salesforce does by default, for faster CI feedback."), ("C", "Run all tests sequentially one at a time forever, since Salesforce does not support any form of parallel Apex test execution."), ("D", "Increase the org's Apex CPU time limit to eliminate the interference between test classes.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Eliminating shared static state and cross-test data dependencies is the standard fix for parallel test interference, and preserving parallel execution (which Salesforce supports by default) keeps CI feedback fast rather than accepting a slow, fully sequential run. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Permanently disabling parallel execution accepts a major CI performance regression instead of fixing the actual root cause (test isolation).\n• C: Salesforce does support parallel Apex test execution by default; the claim that it isn't supported at all is factually incorrect.\n• D: CPU time limits govern execution time per transaction and have nothing to do with shared static variables or inconsistent record state causing test interference.\n\n# Releasing"),
        Question(id: "78", question: "Releasing — An ISV wants to distribute a Salesforce application on AppExchange to hundreds of external subscriber orgs, protect their Apex source code from being viewed or modified by subscribers, and support a formal version upgrade lifecycle. Which packaging approach fits, and what constraint should they plan for post-release?", options: [("A", "Unlocked packages, since they support external distribution and don't require a namespace or IP protection commitment."), ("B", "Managed packages, since they support namespace-based IP protection and a formal subscriber upgrade lifecycle appropriate for AppExchange distribution; the key constraint is that once a version is released, fields, objects, or classes cannot be removed from it — only deprecated — and API names cannot change."), ("C", "Unmanaged packages, since they allow subscribers to freely modify the installed code with no upgrade path needed."), ("D", "A single Change Set repeatedly deployed to each subscriber org whenever an update is needed.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Managed packages provide namespace-based IP protection and a structured version upgrade model designed exactly for AppExchange-style external distribution, with the well-documented constraint that released components can only be deprecated, never outright removed, from a released version. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Unlocked packages don't provide the IP protection an ISV distributing proprietary code externally typically needs, since components aren't obfuscated or locked.\n• C: Unmanaged packages have no formal upgrade path at all, which contradicts the requirement for a subscriber upgrade lifecycle.\n• D: Change Sets require a point-to-point connection between specific orgs and cannot scale to hundreds of independent subscriber orgs; they are not an AppExchange distribution mechanism."),
        Question(id: "79", question: "Releasing — An internal enterprise program wants to decompose a large, monolithic org into independently releasable domains (Billing, Account Management, Reporting) shared across several internal Salesforce orgs, without needing external IP protection or a namespace. Which packaging model fits best?", options: [("A", "Managed packages with a namespace, since namespaces are always required for any form of modular packaging."), ("B", "Unlocked packages, since they support source-driven, modular internal delivery without requiring a namespace, and allow the team to evolve components more freely than a namespace-locked managed package would."), ("C", "Unmanaged packages, since they provide the strongest IP protection for internal domains."), ("D", "A single combined package containing all three domains merged together, to avoid any dependency management.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Unlocked packages are purpose-built for internal, source-driven modular delivery without requiring a namespace, letting the team decompose the monolith into independently releasable domains while retaining more flexibility to evolve than a namespaced managed package. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A namespace is required specifically for managed packages, not for packaging in general; unlocked packages can be created without one.\n• C: Unmanaged packages provide no IP protection at all (subscribers can freely view and modify the code), the opposite of what this option claims.\n• D: Merging all three domains into one package eliminates the independent release cadence and modularity the program is explicitly trying to achieve."),
        Question(id: "80", question: "Releasing — A subscriber org has been using version 3.2.0 of an unlocked package for a year. The publishing team releases version 4.0.0 with a breaking dependency change, alongside 3.3.0 with a minor backward-compatible feature addition. What should the subscriber understand about how these versions apply to their org?", options: [("A", "Both 3.3.0 and 4.0.0 will automatically install in the subscriber org overnight with no action required, since all package updates are automatically pushed."), ("B", "Neither version can ever be installed without completely uninstalling the existing package first and losing all configuration."), ("C", "The subscriber controls their own upgrade timing for unlocked packages; they can adopt 3.3.0 with lower risk since it's backward-compatible, while 4.0.0 (a major version with breaking changes) warrants more careful review and explicit opt-in due to its higher risk of disruption."), ("D", "Major version numbers in Salesforce packaging are purely cosmetic and carry no semantic meaning about compatibility risk.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [2], explanation: "Why correct: Unlocked package subscribers control their own upgrade timing (unlike some automatic managed package update models), and semantic versioning signals real risk differences — a MINOR version is lower-risk to adopt than a MAJOR version with breaking changes, which warrants deliberate review before upgrading. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Unlocked package version upgrades are not automatically pushed to subscriber orgs; the subscribing org's team controls and initiates the upgrade.\n• B: Upgrading to a new package version does not require full uninstallation and loss of configuration in the general case; that would defeat the purpose of package versioning entirely.\n• D: Semantic versioning (MAJOR.MINOR.PATCH) is a meaningful, structured signal about the type and risk level of changes, not just a cosmetic label."),
        Question(id: "81", question: "Releasing — An architect designs a release plan with three parallel development streams (two feature teams and one hotfix team) that must eventually converge into a single production deployment. Which environment sequencing correctly reflects a sound release plan?", options: [("A", "Each stream deploys directly to production independently and simultaneously with no shared integration point."), ("B", "Each stream develops in its own isolated sandbox, merges into a shared Integration sandbox for combined testing, promotes to a Partial Copy QA sandbox for sign-off, then a Partial Copy UAT sandbox for business validation, then a Full Copy Staging sandbox for final pre-production validation, before production; the hotfix stream branches from the production tag and fast-tracks through this pipeline when urgent."), ("C", "All three streams work directly in the Staging Full Copy sandbox from day one, skipping any lower environments."), ("D", "Streams should never converge; each should permanently maintain its own separate production org.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: This sequencing isolates streams during active development, then progressively converges them through shared testing gates of increasing production-fidelity, with a defined fast-track path for genuinely urgent hotfixes — the standard, sound pattern for coordinating parallel streams toward one production release. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Simultaneous independent production deployment with no shared integration testing risks conflicting, uncoordinated changes colliding directly in production.\n• C: Working directly in a Full Copy Staging sandbox from day one skips all the earlier, cheaper testing gates and risks colliding all three streams' unfinished work in the highest-fidelity, least frequently refreshed environment.\n• D: Maintaining permanently separate production orgs for what is described as a single converging release plan contradicts the premise of one eventual production deployment."),
        Question(id: "82", question: "Releasing — A Staging (Full Copy) sandbox has not been refreshed from production in over a year, despite six intervening production releases, and the team just discovered a performance issue in Staging that doesn't reproduce in production. What is the likely cause, and what should the release plan enforce going forward?", options: [("A", "The Staging sandbox is inherently unreliable for performance testing and should be abandoned in favor of testing directly in production."), ("B", "Staging has drifted significantly from production's current metadata and data volume after a year without refresh; the release plan should mandate refreshing Staging from production after every production release to maintain parity for meaningful pre-production validation."), ("C", "Full Copy sandboxes cannot be refreshed more often than once every five years, so this is an unavoidable limitation."), ("D", "The performance issue should simply be ignored, since Staging is not a real environment that matters.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: A year without refresh across six production releases means Staging's metadata and data almost certainly diverged significantly from current production, undermining the performance test's validity; mandating a post-release refresh cadence restores the parity Staging exists to provide. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Staging is specifically valuable for performance testing precisely because of its production-equivalent data volume; the problem is refresh cadence, not the sandbox type's suitability.\n• C: Full Copy sandboxes can be refreshed as often as every 29 days; there's no five-year restriction, and the actual limitation here is process discipline, not platform capability.\n• D: Ignoring a real performance issue discovered pre-production defeats the entire purpose of having a staging gate before customer-facing production impact."),
        Question(id: "83", question: "Releasing — A critical production defect surfaces mid-sprint, while two feature branches are mid-development and not yet merged. The team needs to ship the hotfix quickly without dragging in unfinished feature work, and without losing the fix once the regular release train resumes. What is the correct sequencing?", options: [("A", "Merge one of the in-progress feature branches into the hotfix branch to speed up testing, since more code changes together is more efficient."), ("B", "Branch the hotfix from the last production release tag, fix and validate it there, deploy it to production, then immediately merge the hotfix back into the main/develop branch and both active feature branches, and redeploy it into Integration/QA/UAT so it isn't reverted by the next regular release."), ("C", "Wait for both feature branches to finish before addressing the critical defect, bundling the hotfix with the next full release."), ("D", "Apply the hotfix by editing the compiled code directly in the production org's file system.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Branching from the production tag isolates the hotfix from unfinished work, and immediately merging it back into all active branches and redeploying it to lower environments ensures the next regular release doesn't silently overwrite or revert the fix. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Merging unfinished feature work into the hotfix branch would risk deploying incomplete functionality to production alongside the urgent fix.\n• C: Delaying a critical production defect fix until unrelated feature branches finish leaves customers exposed to the defect far longer than necessary.\n• D: Salesforce has no accessible 'file system' for editing compiled code directly; changes must go through supported deployment mechanisms (Metadata API, CLI, or Setup UI)."),
        Question(id: "84", question: "Releasing — A customer wants to introduce a training sandbox for end users ahead of a major release, but keeps finding that in-progress, unapproved development changes leak into the training environment, confusing trainees. What refresh timing and isolation rule should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Refresh the training sandbox continuously from the Integration sandbox in real time so trainees always see the very latest in-progress code."), ("B", "Refresh the training sandbox only from officially UAT-approved feature sets, timed after UAT sign-off (but with enough lead time before the production go-live for training to occur), and ensure it never receives in-progress development changes directly."), ("C", "Never refresh the training sandbox at all, so it always reflects the very first release the org ever had."), ("D", "Let each individual developer refresh the training sandbox directly from their own personal Developer sandbox whenever convenient.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Restricting the training sandbox to only receive UAT-approved feature sets, refreshed after sign-off but ahead of go-live, isolates trainees from unfinished work while still letting them practice on the near-final feature set before it goes live. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Continuously syncing from Integration means trainees would be exposed to unapproved, in-progress, potentially broken work exactly as described in the problem.\n• C: Never refreshing at all means training becomes permanently disconnected from the current release, defeating its purpose entirely.\n• D: Letting individual developers push their own personal sandbox content into training reintroduces the exact uncontrolled leakage of in-progress work the customer is trying to eliminate."),
        Question(id: "85", question: "Releasing — Choose 2. A regulated financial services customer is deciding on a release cadence for their Salesforce program. They have moderate risk tolerance, a support team that can absorb monthly change volume, but not yet the test automation maturity for continuous deployment. Which two release cadence characteristics best fit this profile? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "A sprint-based cadence (e.g., every two weeks) with predictable, manageable release sizes appropriate for a support team that can absorb regular but not high-frequency change volume."), ("B", "A defined change freeze period before each release, with go/no-go criteria checked before authorizing production deployment, appropriate given moderate risk tolerance in a regulated industry."), ("C", "Full continuous deployment triggered automatically on every merged pull request with no human checkpoint, appropriate for teams without mature test automation."), ("D", "A single release once every two years, regardless of business need, to minimize any change risk whatsoever."), ("E", "No release cadence at all; deployments happen whenever any individual developer personally feels ready.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: A sprint-based cadence matches a support team's absorption capacity without requiring the high automation maturity continuous deployment demands, and a change freeze with go/no-go criteria provides the risk control a regulated financial services customer needs. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Continuous deployment with no human checkpoint requires high test automation maturity, which this customer explicitly does not yet have, making it a poor fit.\n• D: A two-year cadence is disproportionately infrequent for a program with moderate risk tolerance and a support team able to absorb monthly volume, and would badly delay business value delivery.\n• E: No defined cadence at all removes any predictability or governance, which is inappropriate for a regulated industry."),
        Question(id: "86", question: "Releasing — A product team wants to deploy a large new feature to production ahead of its planned announcement date, so the code is ready in advance, but without exposing it to any users until the actual launch date arrives. What release technique should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Wait to deploy the code until the exact minute of the announcement, performing the deployment live during the announcement event."), ("B", "Use feature flagging: deploy the code to production behind a Custom Metadata Type flag (or permission-set-gated visibility) that keeps the feature inactive/hidden until the flag is switched on at the announcement time, decoupling deployment from release."), ("C", "Deploy the feature to a separate, permanent second production org reserved solely for unreleased features."), ("D", "Store the entire feature's Apex code as inactive, uncompiled text files inside a Custom Setting record until launch day.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Feature flagging via Custom Metadata Type (or permission-set-gated component visibility) is the standard Salesforce pattern for decoupling deployment from release, letting code ship to production early while remaining inactive until intentionally activated. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Deploying live during the announcement event introduces unnecessary deployment risk at the highest-visibility, least forgiving moment possible.\n• C: There is no concept of a 'second production org reserved for unreleased features' as a standard Salesforce release pattern; this invents an environment type that doesn't fit standard architecture.\n• D: Apex code must be compiled to exist as a deployable, executable class; it cannot be stored as inactive raw text inside a Custom Setting record and activated later."),
        Question(id: "87", question: "Releasing — A team wants to gradually roll out a new Lightning feature to 5% of users first, monitor for issues, then expand to the full user base if stable, without doing a separate deployment for each rollout phase. What approach best achieves this canary-style rollout in Salesforce?", options: [("A", "Deploy the feature separately to five different sandboxes, one for each 20% of users, and only merge to production once all five have signed off."), ("B", "Deploy the feature once, gated behind a permission set (or custom metadata flag with user/profile conditions) that is initially assigned only to a small pilot group of users; expand the assignment to more users progressively as confidence grows, with no additional deployment needed for each expansion phase."), ("C", "Deploy the feature to 100% of users immediately, then roll it back entirely if any user reports an issue."), ("D", "Ask the 5% pilot group to manually disable the feature themselves using browser settings if they experience problems.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Gating the feature behind a permission set or CMT-driven condition lets the team control exposure by simply expanding user/group assignment, achieving a canary-style progressive rollout from a single deployment with no redeployment needed per phase. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Deploying separately to five sandboxes doesn't control production user exposure at all; sandboxes aren't where end users experience the live rollout.\n• C: Deploying to 100% immediately and rolling back on any complaint is the opposite of a canary rollout, which is specifically designed to limit blast radius before wide exposure.\n• D: There is no browser-level mechanism for end users to disable a Salesforce-side feature; this isn't a real or supported rollback lever."),
        Question(id: "88", question: "Releasing — A multi-org program has Org A providing a shared product catalog API that Org B's Apex integration consumes. The team is planning a coordinated release where both orgs have changes. What sequencing risk must the architect flag, and what is the correct order?", options: [("A", "Deploy to whichever org's release is ready first, since sequencing between orgs never matters for shared APIs."), ("B", "Deploy Org A (the API provider) first, since Org B's integration depends on it; deploying Org B first risks it calling an API contract that doesn't yet exist or has changed incompatibly in the target org."), ("C", "Deploy both orgs at the exact same millisecond using a synchronized script, which eliminates any sequencing consideration entirely."), ("D", "Always deploy the consuming org (Org"), ("B", "first, regardless of dependency direction, since consumers should always go first.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [4], explanation: "Why correct: Since Org B's integration functionally depends on Org A's API, deploying the provider first ensures the contract Org B relies on is in place and stable before the dependent org's changes go live, preventing a broken integration window. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Sequencing absolutely matters when one org's changes depend on another's API contract; ignoring this creates a real risk of calling a non-existent or changed endpoint.\n• C: True millisecond-synchronized deployment across independent orgs isn't a realistic or reliable mechanism, and doesn't remove the underlying dependency risk if something goes wrong in either deployment.\n• D: Deploying the consumer before the provider it depends on directly causes the exact broken-integration risk this scenario is testing for."),
        Question(id: "89", question: "Releasing — A company distributes a shared \"Foundation\" unlocked package containing common utility Apex classes to five internal consuming orgs. A breaking change is needed in Foundation to fix a security issue. What release governance step is most critical before releasing the new Foundation version?", options: [("A", "Release the new Foundation version and let each consuming org discover any breaking changes on their own after installing it."), ("B", "Test the new Foundation version against all five consuming orgs (or representative test environments for each) before general release, given that a breaking change in a shared foundational package can silently break multiple downstream orgs simultaneously, and communicate a clear migration/changelog to all consuming teams."), ("C", "Skip testing entirely for security fixes, since security patches are always assumed to be side-effect-free."), ("D", "Only test the change in the org where the security issue was originally discovered, since the other four orgs use the package identically.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Because a foundational shared package's breaking change can silently propagate failures to every downstream consuming org, validating against all consumers (or representative environments) plus clear changelog communication is the critical governance step before release. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Letting each org discover breaking changes independently after installation risks simultaneous, uncoordinated production incidents across multiple orgs.\n• C: Security fixes can still introduce breaking changes to dependent code; assuming they're always side-effect-free is not a safe assumption.\n• D: Assuming all five orgs use the package identically ignores that each org may have different customizations or usage patterns interacting differently with the change."),
        Question(id: "90", question: "Releasing — A subscriber org has been running an unlocked package for two years without ever upgrading, while the publishing team has released four major versions since. The subscriber now needs a critical new feature only available in the latest major version. What should the architect flag as the key risk before upgrading?", options: [("A", "There is no risk at all; unlocked package upgrades are always fully backward-compatible regardless of how many major versions are skipped."), ("B", "Jumping across four major versions at once likely includes multiple accumulated breaking changes; the team should review the changelog/migration notes for each intervening major version, test thoroughly in a sandbox, and potentially upgrade incrementally rather than assuming a single direct jump is safe."), ("C", "The subscriber should uninstall the package entirely and manually rebuild all its functionality from scratch instead of upgrading."), ("D", "Major version numbers are unrelated to compatibility risk, so skipping versions carries the same risk as upgrading one minor version at a time.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Since MAJOR version increments signal breaking changes, skipping four of them likely means multiple accumulated incompatibilities; reviewing changelogs, testing thoroughly, and considering incremental upgrades is the appropriate risk mitigation before jumping straight to the latest version. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Assuming full backward compatibility across four skipped major versions ignores the very purpose of major version numbering, which explicitly signals breaking changes.\n• C: Uninstalling and manually rebuilding discards the entire benefit of package-based delivery and is a drastically disproportionate response to a version upgrade challenge.\n• D: Major and minor version increments carry fundamentally different risk profiles by design; treating them as equivalent misunderstands semantic versioning.\n\n# Operating"),
        Question(id: "91", question: "Operating — A VP insists a one-field label change be made directly in production right now, bypassing the pipeline, because \"it's trivial and can't possibly cause harm.\" What should the architect explain about the implications of this specific request?", options: [("A", "Agree immediately with no discussion, since label changes never have any downstream impact."), ("B", "Explain that even a seemingly trivial direct production change breaks the source-of-truth alignment between production and source control, bypasses any peer review or CI validation, and creates a configuration drift that must be manually retrieved and back-propagated afterward — the risk isn't the field's complexity, it's the precedent and process bypass."), ("C", "Refuse outright with no explanation, since all direct production changes are against company policy without exception."), ("D", "Make the change directly but tell no one, since documenting it would take longer than the change itself.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: The core implication of any direct production change, regardless of its technical complexity, is that it silently diverges production from the tracked source of truth and skips governance gates; explaining this distinction (process risk vs. technical risk) is the architect's job here. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Even simple label changes can have downstream impacts on translations, integrations parsing label text, or reports, and more importantly break process discipline regardless of technical risk.\n• C: A blanket refusal with no explanation misses the opportunity to educate the stakeholder on the actual risk and doesn't address the legitimate need for occasional emergency processes.\n• D: Making an undocumented change is worse than either approving or declining transparently, since it guarantees future configuration drift with no trace of what happened."),
        Question(id: "92", question: "Operating — A stakeholder demands an emergency production fix for a customer-facing outage impacting revenue, and there is no time for the standard multi-day change window. What is the correct way to weigh and act on this request?", options: [("A", "Always follow the standard multi-day process regardless of business impact, since deviating from process is never acceptable under any circumstances."), ("B", "Quantify the actual business impact of not fixing it now versus the risk of an unreviewed direct change, and if it meets the org's defined emergency criteria, use the sanctioned emergency change process: a pre-authorized approver signs off, the minimal necessary fix is made, and it is retrieved into source control and back-propagated within a fixed window afterward."), ("C", "Let any available developer make the fix immediately with no approval and no plan to document or back-propagate it later."), ("D", "Escalate to the full standing CAB and wait for their next regularly scheduled monthly meeting before taking any action.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Weighing quantified business impact against process risk, then using a pre-defined emergency change path with pre-authorized approval and mandatory follow-up documentation, is exactly the balance a mature ALM program strikes between urgency and governance. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Rigidly following a multi-day process during a genuine revenue-impacting outage ignores that emergency paths exist precisely for scenarios like this.\n• C: Letting anyone make an unapproved, undocumented change reintroduces the exact accountability gap that a sanctioned emergency process is designed to prevent.\n• D: Waiting for the next monthly CAB meeting during an active revenue-impacting outage is a wholly inappropriate response time for the stated urgency."),
        Question(id: "93", question: "Operating — Following a legitimate emergency change made directly in production last week under an approved fast-track process, the change still has not been retrieved into source control or propagated to lower sandboxes. What operational risk does this create, and what should happen next?", options: [("A", "No risk exists, since the change was already approved through the emergency process, so no further action is needed."), ("B", "The uncommitted change creates ongoing configuration drift risk — the next regular deployment from source control could silently overwrite or conflict with it; the team should immediately retrieve the change, commit it to a branch with context on why it was made, get it peer reviewed, and deploy it to all lower environments to resync them with production."), ("C", "The change should be reverted entirely now that a week has passed, regardless of whether the original issue is still resolved."), ("D", "Lower sandboxes should be permanently excluded from ever receiving this specific change.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: An emergency change that hasn't been retrieved and back-propagated is exactly the seed of configuration drift; the next regular deployment could silently undo it, so completing the retrieve-commit-review-propagate cycle promptly is the necessary follow-up step regardless of the change having been pre-approved. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Approval for making the emergency change doesn't eliminate the separate, ongoing risk of it never being captured in source control.\n• C: Reverting a fix that resolved a real production issue, simply because a week passed, would reintroduce the original problem for no good reason.\n• D: Permanently excluding the change from lower sandboxes guarantees those environments never match production and any future testing there will be invalid."),
        Question(id: "94", question: "Operating — An org compare tool reveals that production contains several custom fields and one Apex trigger that do not exist anywhere in the team's Git repository, with no record of who added them or why. What is the correct reconciliation approach?", options: [("A", "Immediately delete all unrecognized components from production without further investigation, since anything not in source control must be unauthorized."), ("B", "Investigate each drifted item to determine whether it's an intentional, legitimate change that was never captured, or truly unauthorized; for legitimate items, retrieve and commit them to source control with documentation; for unauthorized items, follow the org's change management process to evaluate and remediate."), ("C", "Add a note to the README file listing the drifted components as \"known issues\" and take no further action."), ("D", "Disable all Apex triggers org-wide until the investigation concludes, regardless of business impact.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Configuration drift reconciliation requires distinguishing legitimate-but-uncaptured changes (which should be retrieved and documented) from truly unauthorized ones (which need remediation via change management) — deleting or ignoring drift without this investigation risks removing needed functionality or leaving a real gap unaddressed. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Deleting components without investigation risks breaking legitimate functionality that simply was never captured in source control, rather than being genuinely unauthorized.\n• C: Documenting drift as a permanent \"known issue\" without remediation leaves the underlying governance gap and audit risk unresolved indefinitely.\n• D: Disabling all triggers org-wide is a drastic, business-disrupting overreaction disproportionate to investigating a handful of drifted components."),
        Question(id: "95", question: "Operating — Choose 2. After repeated incidents of admins making unauthorized direct changes in production, leadership wants structural controls to prevent recurrence, not just better documentation after the fact. Which two governance controls would most directly prevent future unauthorized direct production changes? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Restrict 'Modify All Data' and 'Customize Application' permissions to a dedicated CI/CD service account, removing them from individual admin profiles so production changes can only flow through the pipeline."), ("B", "Configure monitoring/alerting on direct production logins and configuration changes not attributable to the CI/CD service account, so unauthorized changes are detected quickly."), ("C", "Send a company-wide email reminding admins not to make direct changes, with no technical enforcement."), ("D", "Grant every admin permanent 'Modify All Data' access but ask them to self-report any changes voluntarily."), ("E", "Increase the number of admins with full production access to spread out the workload.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Removing broad modification permissions from individual admin profiles (forcing changes through the pipeline) and adding monitoring for out-of-band changes are structural, technically enforced controls that directly prevent and detect unauthorized direct changes, rather than relying on voluntary compliance. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: A reminder email with no technical enforcement is exactly the kind of non-structural control leadership explicitly said they want to move beyond.\n• D: Granting permanent broad access and relying on voluntary self-reporting preserves the exact capability and incentive structure that caused the original incidents.\n• E: Increasing the number of admins with full access expands, rather than restricts, the surface area for unauthorized direct changes."),
        Question(id: "96", question: "Operating — A holding company operates six semi-autonomous Salesforce orgs, each maintaining its own copy of a shared \"corporate standards\" Apex utility library, manually copy-pasted between orgs whenever updated. Updates are inconsistent, and three orgs are now running different, diverged versions. What structural approach should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Continue manual copy-paste updates, but ask each admin to be more careful this time."), ("B", "Package the shared utility library as a versioned unlocked package that each of the six orgs installs as a dependency; when the library is updated, a new package version is released, and each org's team explicitly controls when to adopt it, with governance defining ownership and a communicated changelog for each update."), ("C", "Merge all six orgs into a single org immediately to eliminate the need for a shared library entirely."), ("D", "Have each org maintain its own permanently separate fork of the utility library with no attempt at consistency going forward.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Packaging the shared library as a versioned unlocked package installed by each org gives consistent, trackable updates with clear versioning and changelogs, while letting each org control its own upgrade timing — directly solving the inconsistent manual copy-paste problem. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Asking for more care without changing the underlying manual, unversioned distribution mechanism doesn't address the structural cause of the inconsistency.\n• C: Merging six semi-autonomous orgs is an enormous, high-risk undertaking disproportionate to fixing a shared utility library distribution problem, and isn't what was asked.\n• D: Permanently forking with no consistency effort abandons any hope of the six orgs sharing a common, maintainable standard, which was the original intent of the shared library."),
        Question(id: "97", question: "Operating — Choose 2. A multi-org release requires updating a shared package installed in three consuming orgs, plus corresponding changes in each org's local Apex that calls the package's new methods. Which two coordination practices best reduce the risk of a broken cross-org rollout? (Choose 2)", options: [("A", "Sequence deployments so the shared package version is installed in each consuming org before that org's local Apex changes referencing new package methods are deployed."), ("B", "Run integration tests that exercise the actual cross-org interface (the specific new package methods and their expected behavior) after each org's deployment, not just isolated unit tests within a single org."), ("C", "Deploy all three consuming orgs' local Apex changes first, and install the updated shared package afterward in each org, since order doesn't matter for package dependencies."), ("D", "Have each of the three orgs independently decide, with no communication between teams, exactly when to deploy their respective changes."), ("E", "Skip integration testing entirely, relying solely on each org's existing unit test suite that predates the package update.")], questionType: .multiSelect, correctIndices: [0, 1], explanation: "Why correct: Installing the updated package before the local Apex that depends on its new methods avoids calling functionality that doesn't yet exist in that org, and integration tests targeting the actual cross-org interface catch problems that isolated unit tests, written before the update, cannot. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• C: Deploying local Apex that calls new package methods before the package itself is installed would cause compilation or runtime errors referencing non-existent methods.\n• D: Uncoordinated, independent timing across the three orgs risks exactly the kind of sequencing and interface mismatch this coordination effort is meant to prevent.\n• E: Existing unit tests written before the package update cannot validate behavior against the new methods being introduced, leaving the actual change unverified."),
        Question(id: "98", question: "Operating — Two weeks after a production release, a spike in Apex exception emails reveals a previously undetected edge case is now failing in production, though nothing looked wrong immediately after deployment. What operational monitoring gap does this reveal, and what should be improved?", options: [("A", "Nothing needs to change, since a two-week delay before problems surface is considered completely normal and expected for every release."), ("B", "Extend structured post-release monitoring (Apex exception email review, error log/event monitoring queries, and key business process completion tracking) beyond the first 24-48 hours for releases touching higher-risk logic, since some edge cases only surface under specific data conditions that take time to occur naturally."), ("C", "Disable Apex exception email notifications entirely, since they are apparently unreliable if the issue wasn't caught immediately."), ("D", "Roll back the entire release two weeks later regardless of whether the specific edge case has since been identified and fixable in isolation.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Standard 24-48 hour post-release monitoring can miss edge cases that only manifest under rare data conditions; extending structured monitoring for higher-risk changes, and treating exception email spikes as an active signal, closes this specific detection gap. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: A two-week-delayed edge case is a real monitoring gap worth addressing, not something to dismiss as universally normal and unworthy of any process change.\n• C: Disabling the very notification mechanism that successfully surfaced the issue removes the team's visibility rather than improving it.\n• D: A full rollback two weeks later is a disproportionate response if the specific edge case can be identified and fixed with a targeted patch instead."),
        Question(id: "99", question: "Operating — A company with three connected Salesforce orgs wants a single, unambiguous decision framework for who approves changes to a shared component used by all three, given each org has its own local governance team. What governance structure should the architect recommend?", options: [("A", "Let whichever org's team requests a change to the shared component unilaterally approve and deploy it themselves without input from the other two orgs."), ("B", "Define clear cross-org ownership of the shared component (which team owns its quality/backward compatibility), require higher-level, cross-org approval for changes to it given the multi-org blast radius, and mandate the change be tested across all consuming orgs before release, with a distributed changelog."), ("C", "Dissolve all three orgs' local governance teams and centralize every single governance decision, including unrelated local-only changes, under one committee."), ("D", "Avoid ever changing the shared component again, freezing it permanently regardless of business need.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Clear ownership, elevated cross-org approval proportional to the shared component's multi-org blast radius, and mandatory cross-org testing with communicated changelogs is the standard governance pattern for shared release artifacts spanning multiple orgs. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Unilateral approval by whichever org happens to request the change ignores the impact on the other two consuming orgs entirely, risking uncoordinated breakage.\n• C: Centralizing every decision, including unrelated local-only changes, is disproportionate overreach beyond what's needed to govern just the shared component.\n• D: Permanently freezing the shared component regardless of legitimate business need is an extreme, unsustainable response that blocks necessary evolution indefinitely."),
        Question(id: "100", question: "Operating — A customer wants to detect production issues proactively rather than waiting for user complaints after a release. They currently have no visibility beyond checking Setup manually once a day. What combination of tools should the architect recommend for ongoing post-release operational monitoring?", options: [("A", "Rely exclusively on user-submitted support tickets as the sole detection mechanism for production issues going forward."), ("B", "Combine Apex Exception Email notifications for immediate alerting on unhandled exceptions, Salesforce Event Monitoring (where licensed) for detailed queryable event logs, the Setup Deployment Status history for deployment-level review, and targeted debug logs for specific users when diagnosing a reported issue."), ("C", "Check Setup manually twice a day instead of once, with no other tooling changes."), ("D", "Disable all error logging to reduce noise, since fewer visible errors implies a healthier org.")], questionType: .singleSelect, correctIndices: [1], explanation: "Why correct: Combining automated exception alerting, detailed event-level monitoring, deployment history review, and targeted debug logging gives proactive, layered visibility into production health, directly replacing the reactive, complaint-driven detection the customer currently has. Why each wrong answer fails:\n\n• A: Waiting for user complaints is precisely the reactive detection model the customer explicitly wants to move away from.\n• C: Doubling the frequency of manual Setup checks without adding any structured tooling doesn't meaningfully improve detection speed or coverage.\n• D: Disabling error logging removes the very signal needed to detect issues; fewer visible errors would reflect reduced visibility, not improved org health."),
    ]
}
